WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:45.578
Tim Ferriss: Chris, welcome to&nbsp;
Austin. Nice to see you, man.

00:00:45.578 --> 00:00:47.067
Chris Beresford-Hill: Nice to see&nbsp;
you too. Thanks for having me.&nbsp;

00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:53.760
Tim Ferriss: Absolutely. So we are going to&nbsp;
bob and weave, and do some improv jazz in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:59.080
this conversation. We're going to wind all&nbsp;
over the place. Let's start with a basic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:03.400
which is how did you land your first job?
Chris Beresford-Hill: My first job was at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:09.760
very cool, very trendy ad agency in Boston called&nbsp;
Modernista. And I convinced them to let me be an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:15.880
unpaid intern, because they were so small they&nbsp;
didn't know how to say no to me coming in and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:21.640
offering to work for free. So I just graduated&nbsp;
college and I got really lucky, because there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:25.240
was kind of no high-speed internet or anything.&nbsp;
I just found them in the phone book literally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:30.880
and I walked around and it was this cool office.
By virtue of that, I found my way there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.280
and they let me be an unpaid intern&nbsp;
and they let me write website copy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:41.360
very Web 1.0 copy for General Motors for the&nbsp;
Hummer truck, when that was brought back with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:49.120
the H1 and the H2. And I decided this was&nbsp;
my dream. Maybe write a ton of web copy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:53.440
and that was making me really excited. Maybe do a&nbsp;
print ad back when there was magazines everywhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.720
maybe put an idea on the back cover of a magazine&nbsp;
and that would really be massively fulfilling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.800
So as soon as I got that internship, I had to&nbsp;
figure out how to get hired, but they weren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:06.298
hiring. And it was very, at the time —
Tim Ferriss: To go from unpaid to paid.&nbsp;

00:02:06.298 --> 00:02:10.000
Chris Beresford-Hill: Unpaid to paid, barely paid.&nbsp;
But at that time, and we could touch on this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.760
it was much more competitive to get into the&nbsp;
ad biz, because there weren't as many creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:26.240
options for a career. So we had an assignment&nbsp;
for the Dallas Mavericks 2002 NBA program,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.800
like the magazine that people would buy when&nbsp;
they went to Mavericks games. And at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:37.600
Mark Cuban was this very — besides just being&nbsp;
Mr. Shark Tank, he was this very outspoken NBA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.920
owner. He was always criticizing the league and&nbsp;
the officials and he was always getting fined,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:45.960
and that was a big theme. So I wrote an ad, and&nbsp;
it wasn't a particularly good one, it was okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:51.040
but I wrote "Even Mark Cuban has nothing bad&nbsp;
to say about it," with a picture of the truck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.600
which I thought was clever. I don't know&nbsp;
if it really stands the test of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:59.240
but I knew that getting a billionaire to give you&nbsp;
the rights to their name was probably something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:02.400
that had to be thought through, where there's&nbsp;
probably some people that would weigh in on that.&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:08.520
And I remember this urban legend, again, very&nbsp;
like Web 1.0, which was that Mark Cuban had an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:14.640
email address that if you could find it and&nbsp;
you emailed him, he wrote everybody back. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:20.120
I kind of put together a very short, concise&nbsp;
email pleading my case. "I'm an unpaid intern;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:25.080
this would mean so much if you would let me put&nbsp;
this ad into the world." And I made a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:32.400
PDF and I attached it. And like true to magic,&nbsp;
about an hour later, he just wrote back and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.440
just said, "Go for it. -M." And so —
Tim Ferriss: You summoned the genie.&nbsp;

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:41.710
Chris Beresford-Hill: He was there for it.&nbsp;
Maybe it was because it was an ad about him.&nbsp;

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:42.840
Tim Ferriss: He was waiting.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah. Maybe there was other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.440
emails he didn't want to write back to, but maybe&nbsp;
this worked for him. And so I remember I printed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:51.880
out the ad and then I printed out the email from&nbsp;
him, and I put it underneath and I showed my boss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:56.560
Lance, and he laughed at that ad and then said&nbsp;
that the requisite, "We're never going to be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:02.680
to do it, but thanks, kid." And then he turned the&nbsp;
page and there was the email. And I think that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:06.400
more than any creative idea I'd ever shown him,&nbsp;
and I was doing my best to show him everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:10.000
I think I showed him that I was going&nbsp;
to solve every problem with creativity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:16.400
not just the assignment. And so we ran that&nbsp;
underwhelming ad, but that was enough for him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:23.907
to give me a job offer for 22 1/2 thousand dollars&nbsp;
a year, which was the best job offer I ever got.&nbsp;

00:04:23.907 --> 00:04:31.080
Tim Ferriss: So many follow-up questions.&nbsp;
So the first is, and you may not recall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:35.818
but do you have any idea what the subject&nbsp;
line was, or the gist of the subject line?&nbsp;

00:04:35.818 --> 00:04:40.040
Chris Beresford-Hill: You know what, I don't&nbsp;
remember the subject line, but I'll tell you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:47.240
this. I think it was good and considered, because&nbsp;
to this day, I receive a lot of email that's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:52.880
from people I'm looking to hear from, and I know&nbsp;
the power of a subject line. So I think very hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:58.560
on these things. Everything is the communication.&nbsp;
So, for example, when I worked at TBWA\Chiat\Day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:04.720
I would sometimes cold email clients, we'd&nbsp;
have an idea for Audible or an idea for Ikea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:09.400
and I would find the client and I would reach&nbsp;
out, and I figured out the perfect email subject&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:14.707
which was "Hi, from Apple's ad agency," because&nbsp;
TBWA is Apple's ad agency. And my response —&nbsp;

00:05:14.707 --> 00:05:15.240
Tim Ferriss: That's strong.
Chris Beresford-Hill: — rate on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:20.960
that was killer. So I've definitely learned&nbsp;
that your first impression is kind of your only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:25.520
impression. So maybe I appealed to his kindness.&nbsp;
Maybe it was desperate, desperate, unpaid intern.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.000
I don't know what, but it was something.
Tim Ferriss: Maybe you appealed to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.898
level of get-shit-done, and he&nbsp;
appreciated the chutzpah, and —&nbsp;

00:05:32.898 --> 00:05:34.400
Chris Beresford-Hill: I hope so.
Tim Ferriss: — it would seem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:36.880
be the case if he gave you permission.
Chris Beresford-Hill: So if I ever see him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:40.120
I'll say a very big thank you.
Tim Ferriss: He may end up hearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:46.280
through the podcast. So coming back to&nbsp;
the phone book, becoming an unpaid intern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:52.818
why did you choose to focus — because&nbsp;
the phone book has a lot of entries.&nbsp;

00:05:52.818 --> 00:05:53.720
Chris Beresford-Hill: That's right.
Tim Ferriss: There's a lot of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.720 --> 00:06:02.098
types of businesses. Why did you choose to pursue&nbsp;
this particular company? Or at least sector?&nbsp;

00:06:02.098 --> 00:06:07.382
Chris Beresford-Hill: Everything's luck. You can&nbsp;
be prepared for it, you can have some gifts, but —&nbsp;

00:06:07.382 --> 00:06:08.120
Tim Ferriss: You need some luck.
Chris Beresford-Hill: — you're an idiot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:13.480
if you don't think that chance and fate and&nbsp;
all those things. Because there were a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:17.960
of ad agencies in Boston, and I was sure I&nbsp;
wanted to be an advertising creative person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:23.040
but again, Googling wasn't a thing to do. The&nbsp;
computer with the internet was at the library,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:28.960
and I think it was like yellowpages.com. And I&nbsp;
think Modernista was the name of the agency. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:33.880
that's kind of in the middle. That's not the first&nbsp;
one. I think I tried Arnold advertising in Boston,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:39.120
and they didn't want to meet with me. I think I&nbsp;
just worked my way down, and I think it was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:44.080
the right place, right time, where they were like,&nbsp;
"Yeah, we'll take free labor." So it was really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:48.000
you couldn't look up what the client lists were.
Tim Ferriss: Did you have a pitch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:50.360
or was it "I will do anything if you let me in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:53.280
the door?" Or was it more specific?
Chris Beresford-Hill: It was "I will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:58.160
do anything," because I had nothing to offer.&nbsp;
And I think later, I would get my second job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:01.640
and I would go to someone, and now&nbsp;
I'm a copywriter, and I would say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:08.200
"I will write on your accounts in my spare time,&nbsp;
if you want." You got to offer whatever you have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:12.000
but I started out with nothing to offer. So I&nbsp;
think I knew I was free labor, and I said I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.000
get coffee, and I did, and I got a lot of coffee.
Tim Ferriss: We may come back to that. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:23.600
a lot of power in being willing to do the small&nbsp;
things, because a lot of folks feel like they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:28.400
too good for it or too qualified, but we may come&nbsp;
back to that. I want to ask you for the program,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:33.120
and maybe you already said this and I missed it,&nbsp;
coming up with the "Even Mark Cuban has nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:38.680
bad to say about this particular car." Did they&nbsp;
give that to you as an assignment or is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:42.200
something you just did of your own volition?
Chris Beresford-Hill: No, I think I graduated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:48.080
from website copy to the smallest of&nbsp;
creative tasks. And so I think there was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:54.080
you could write the welcome signs for the GM&nbsp;
convention and try to make that clever, and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:58.400
could write — so there's all kinds of little&nbsp;
things that weren't like the big-brand stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:02.440
and they weren't the big TV spot or any — and&nbsp;
that's kind of where all the focus was and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:07.080
kind of had to work your way up to those things.
Tim Ferriss: Work your way up. So you said that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:10.400
you have to consider luck, I'm paraphrasing&nbsp;
here, which is of course true. There's certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:13.880
things you can do to increase your surface&nbsp;
area for luck to stick to them. Borrowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:19.160
that concept from somebody else, I don't&nbsp;
know the attribution. And you didn't get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:25.640
yes from the first few spots or the first few&nbsp;
companies you contacted. Did Modernista end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:35.243
up being a particularly good fit or particularly&nbsp;
influential in a way you could not have predicted?&nbsp;

00:08:35.243 --> 00:08:37.920
Chris Beresford-Hill: It gave me — so&nbsp;
interestingly, when I kind of found my own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:42.800
quote-unquote, "voice." Modernista was one&nbsp;
of these ad agencies that was very stylish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:49.560
very European, everything was very cool, and they&nbsp;
would put out work that was very avant-garde. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:55.480
think I later became a little bit more mass, a&nbsp;
little bit more comedic in the work that kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.480 --> 00:09:02.920
came out of me. But what I got at Modernista&nbsp;
is the owner was a guy named Lance Jensen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:09.160
and he, for any ad nerds, you know exactly who&nbsp;
that person is, because he had written one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:13.280
the best advertising lines of all time, which&nbsp;
is "On the road of life, there are passengers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:19.560
and there are drivers. Drivers wanted for VW."
And so he had created that campaign while working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:24.040
in Boston at an agency called Arnold, and he'd&nbsp;
done all these famous ads. I don't know if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:28.320
remember, there was an ad, I don't know what&nbsp;
the name was, but it's that "Da Da Da" song,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:34.000
and a couple of guys just killing time driving&nbsp;
around in their VW Golf, and I think they pick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:37.160
up a chair, and they drive around a little&nbsp;
further, they realize the chair smells because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:41.400
they picked it up off the street and they let it&nbsp;
back out and they drive around and the line said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:46.600
"The VW Golf. Everything you need for your life,&nbsp;
or your complete lack thereof." Things like that.&nbsp;

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:51.920
So there's all these amazing ads and there was&nbsp;
years and years of them. And so he had struck out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:56.880
on his own and opened this ad agency, unbeknownst&nbsp;
to me, Modernista. So when I walked in, they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:03.240
accrued maybe 20, 25 people there. But I didn't&nbsp;
know that I was going to effectively be interning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:09.640
for one of the best, most poetic writers in the&nbsp;
industry ever. So that was dumb luck. But what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:14.400
I got out of that is the one thing you cannot&nbsp;
quite step back and get from the start is your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:21.920
taste level. At the end of the day, it is a skill,&nbsp;
it is a muscle, it is an ability, but ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:26.040
you're making choices on the ideas you're&nbsp;
coming up with and the ideas you're approving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:30.640
and putting forward. And that's all on taste.
And I don't think there's anyone on the planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:35.280
that says they have bad taste. Everyone thinks&nbsp;
they have good taste. And so I literally stumbled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:41.640
on the doorstep of just one of the most thoughtful&nbsp;
emotional writers ever. So I learned my taste from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:46.400
him, and I learned the bar and the standard and I&nbsp;
learned what was acceptable and making everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:50.800
feel brand new and interesting. He could write&nbsp;
a line about a car, and it would make a — we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:54.360
know what a car is, but he could make a car&nbsp;
sound like this really exciting thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:59.200
you really needed to get one of. And that's&nbsp;
the art of it. So I learned that from him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:04.467
And the best gift you can ever get is a first&nbsp;
boss that has great taste or high standards.&nbsp;

00:11:04.467 --> 00:11:08.000
Tim Ferriss: We will come back to taste, almost&nbsp;
certainly, but I'm going to bookmark that. I'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:12.560
say a few things, and this is context that&nbsp;
you did not have before we started recording,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:20.560
but as I began to explore entrepreneurship, all&nbsp;
of the books, almost all of the books that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:27.800
first read were on copywriting. So Caples&nbsp;
and all of the classics. I also bought as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:34.400
many books as I could possibly find and afford on&nbsp;
print advertising, because at the time, this is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:41.800
let's call it 2000, 2001, print was still a&nbsp;
thing and it is still a thing to a lesser and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:47.720
lesser level, but I was going to be doing a lot&nbsp;
of direct-response advertising in magazines. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:52.480
I had swipe files; I had a three-ring binder&nbsp;
where I collected various advertisements. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:58.960
also bookmarked a lot of advertisements, one of&nbsp;
which was the VW campaign that you just mentioned.&nbsp;

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:09.040
And I am endlessly fascinated by copy because it's&nbsp;
effectively, at its best, I think, sort of poetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:15.840
mind control. The idea that I'm making these&nbsp;
sounds that are coming out of my face that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:22.240
instantaneously registering semi-instantaneously&nbsp;
through your senses into your brain and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:26.120
facilitating thought is pretty wild when you sit&nbsp;
down and think about it. And no, I'm not on drugs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:32.560
people, at the moment, just to be clear.
I suppose my next question is actually bridging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:41.040
the bookends of where we started, which is this&nbsp;
cold email to Mark Cuban, and then an email that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:47.840
you sent to my team. The reason I think this&nbsp;
is perhaps fun to unpack is that a friend of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:55.160
mine asked me earlier today, she said, "How many&nbsp;
guests have you booked who have cold emailed you?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.160 --> 00:13:02.680
I thought, and I said, "I honestly can't even&nbsp;
think of three. But your email worked, which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.680 --> 00:13:07.760
one would hope, given your track record." And I&nbsp;
was like, "Okay, so this guy knows how to play the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:11.880
game, and he knows how to get my attention.&nbsp;
He knows how to get it surfaced, at least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:16.880
to my attention, and now we're sitting here." So&nbsp;
when you thought about that email, how did you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:22.240
think about crafting that, or any email like it?
Chris Beresford-Hill: Honest answer. I think I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:28.360
have the muscle memory now, so I don't think I had&nbsp;
to really sit down. I think I made the connection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:32.200
I thought, "Boy, I really want to talk about this&nbsp;
with a broader audience, and I want to get more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:37.680
people thinking about advertising, and I want&nbsp;
people to think about it as a career." So I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:44.080
that thought, and then I found my way to you via&nbsp;
our mutual friend Zach, because that's part of it.&nbsp;

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:49.880
The muscle memory for me is not only coming up&nbsp;
with ideas. The other muscle memory is actioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:54.760
and executing them. One thing to have all&nbsp;
these great thoughts. I know people that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:59.480
are so much smarter and more interesting&nbsp;
than me, but it's also pairing that with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.480 --> 00:14:06.520
this knowing how to do it. So I hope I had a&nbsp;
good subject line, and I hope I was brief and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.520 --> 00:14:11.480
didn't oversell it, because no one likes that,&nbsp;
and I think that's probably what I would've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.480 --> 00:14:16.680
stuck to. I think I was kind of doing what I do.
Tim Ferriss: I have your email in front of me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:25.120
so let me share my perception of my read of the&nbsp;
ingredients. First thing is you get to the point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:34.000
It's not six paragraphs of meandering life story.&nbsp;
You sort of established that you know the podcast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:40.720
very quickly, within the first two sentences. And&nbsp;
you mentioned, very quickly, your credibility. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:47.240
21 years chasing the dream, to much success and&nbsp;
far more failure. Okay. What kind of failure? So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:52.520
you're prompting sort of questions. Immediately,&nbsp;
sizzle reel of some of my career highlights, short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:56.520
sizzle reel, which you can accomplish in different&nbsp;
ways, by the way. It could just be a few bullets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.520 --> 00:15:01.400
but credibility up front, in other words. And then&nbsp;
thanks for entertaining this. Here's some macro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:08.120
topics we could talk about. So immediately getting&nbsp;
