including several that I would want answered,&nbsp;
not just for my audience, but for myself.&nbsp;

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:16.800
So I would say establishing credibility, yes,&nbsp;
you have the connection, but that actually is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:23.440
just table stakes. And particularly if I'm&nbsp;
not extremely close to the person making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:27.640
the introduction. If it's someone close, I know&nbsp;
they're putting their social capital at risk with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.640 --> 00:15:37.360
me. So it gets, in some cases, elevated by someone&nbsp;
on my team. But it's very rare that something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.360 --> 00:15:44.800
like this will convert. But I was interested in&nbsp;
exploring, as you led to in the probably second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:48.880
paragraph of your email, not just the successes&nbsp;
but also the failures. But first, we're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:55.258
to look at the nurturing and development of your&nbsp;
ability to get things done on the execution side.&nbsp;

00:15:55.258 --> 00:15:59.680
Chris Beresford-Hill: Well before that, by the&nbsp;
way, I think in that email, and maybe the big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.680 --> 00:16:04.640
thing about it, because we also, when we pitch&nbsp;
as an agency, we show up with 10 people and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

