00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:41.160
or the third person. So I'm just pointing out&nbsp;
that it was well crafted, and a good investment.&nbsp;

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:46.458
All right. Father-son trip to California. I'm&nbsp;
going to use that and let you run with it.&nbsp;

00:17:46.458 --> 00:17:51.600
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yes, this is something, it&nbsp;
is a formative experience that I only really put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:57.240
together a couple years ago on Father's Day when&nbsp;
I kind of felt like I wanted to tell my father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.240 --> 00:18:02.120
how inspiring he is for me. Because your dad's&nbsp;
your dad, your mom's your mom. I don't often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.120 --> 00:18:08.000
take a step back and let him know the things he&nbsp;
showed me when I was young, how much I value them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:14.040
and how much they helped shape me. So when I&nbsp;
was 10 years old, we took a father-son trip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.040 --> 00:18:21.720
to California, and it was the first time I'd ever&nbsp;
been to the West Coast. And my father's a teacher.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:25.400
It probably would've been a family vacation,&nbsp;
but I think maybe it was like a "Let's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.400 --> 00:18:29.800
send the two of you." And on the docket was we&nbsp;
were going to go to Universal Studios and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.800 --> 00:18:33.240
we were going to go to a waterpark and then we&nbsp;
were going to go to the beach or what have you.&nbsp;

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:37.840
Obviously, Universal Studios was the big ticket&nbsp;
item because those were really expensive, and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:43.960
was the highlight. We go to Universal Studios and&nbsp;
my mind was blown, because I had never — I grew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.960 --> 00:18:48.760
up a little bit of a shy, reclusive kid. So I did&nbsp;
kind of — if I wasn't out playing with the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:53.840
kids, I was watching TV, I was watching movies. So&nbsp;
that world was extra important to me as a child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.840 --> 00:18:59.840
and I'd never been anywhere near where these&nbsp;
actors were, where these things happened. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:07.160
so we took the tram tour and I saw the actual&nbsp;
house from Psycho, and the real Hill Valley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.160 --> 00:19:13.720
Town Center from Back to the Future, which was my&nbsp;
favorite movie ever. And even the empty water tank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.720 --> 00:19:18.280
that was — I still remember this, that it was&nbsp;
also at cross purposes a parking lot, but had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.280 --> 00:19:22.800
a giant painting of an infinite ocean behind it,&nbsp;
and that's where they filmed the water scenes for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:27.240
Jaws and a dozen other movies and all this stuff.
