00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:31.680
ways, I've been kind of chasing that rush&nbsp;
to production into sets and to where it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:35.640
happening ever since. But I mean, the guy&nbsp;
walking past the tram with the paint bucket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:39.947
I wanted to be him, because he was doing&nbsp;
something. So anyway, it just blew my mind.&nbsp;

00:19:39.947 --> 00:19:42.200
Tim Ferriss: How old were you at the time?
Chris Beresford-Hill: I was probably nine or 10.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.760 --> 00:19:47.640
So it blew my mind. So we go home, we go to the&nbsp;
hotel and call my mom and report on the day and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.640 --> 00:19:52.720
said, "Look, I know we can't go back, but I would&nbsp;
give anything to go back." And that was that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:59.440
and I appreciated it. And then the next morning,&nbsp;
my father wakes me up maybe at 5:00 or 6:00 a.m.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:02.240
which is really early to a nine-year-old.
Tim Ferriss: It's pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:04.120
early for a lot of people.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Pretty early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.120 --> 00:20:08.160
for a lot of people. And we get in the rental&nbsp;
car, and I don't really know where we're going,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.160 --> 00:20:13.600
and we drive 20, 30 minutes, and we&nbsp;
wind up in some kind of a strip mall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.600 --> 00:20:17.520
and we go into a storefront, and I&nbsp;
remember this, and there's a row of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:20.800
chairs and an old-school projector, I&nbsp;
guess at the time it wasn't old school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:24.080
it was just a projector. It was 1990.
Tim Ferriss: Like overhead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:25.960
transparencies or something like that.
Chris Beresford-Hill: All that stuff, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:32.200
the sheets and — and it was a four-hour timeshare&nbsp;
meeting for a new development in Anaheim. It was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:36.720
hard sell. They were coming in, they were talking&nbsp;
about these are going to go fast, and people would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.720 --> 00:20:41.400
raise their hand and get up and buy into it. And&nbsp;
then after they left the room, they would lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.400 --> 00:20:45.400
the price. I was worried. I didn't know what was&nbsp;
going on. I was worried my father was going to buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.400 --> 00:20:49.760
one of these things. And so after this whole four&nbsp;
hours is over, we kind of go to the front to sign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:56.680
out, and we were rewarded with two super crisp day&nbsp;
passes to Universal Studios. So my father figured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.680 --> 00:21:01.880
out that that was another way to get access.
And so we burned rubber, got to the park,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.880 --> 00:21:07.960
and we rode the back lot tour until they literally&nbsp;
kicked us out of Universal Studios. And I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.960 --> 00:21:14.240
that was what we're getting at with this story&nbsp;
was this is — my father kind of taught me the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:18.760
definition of creativity, which is really just&nbsp;
looking at any situation, finding another way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:23.400
because he didn't overextend himself and buy&nbsp;
tickets he couldn't afford. He didn't get mad or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.400 --> 00:21:28.440
upset that he couldn't get them. He bypassed all&nbsp;
of that and he was just always going to figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:34.640
another way, and that's who he is and that's how&nbsp;
he lives his life. And my father is a guy who has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.640 --> 00:21:41.680
never let his situation ever determine where&nbsp;
he was going to go. And it rubbed off on me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:47.080
and in a number of other ways, it kind of&nbsp;
made me a little audacious or precocious.&nbsp;

00:21:47.080 --> 00:21:52.800
And so I was this very shy kid. I was very little&nbsp;
and we moved around a lot. So I was kind of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:55.858
every couple of years I was the new kid.
Tim Ferriss: Why did you move around a lot?&nbsp;

00:21:55.858 --> 00:21:59.040
