00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:21.920
sequentially immediately after that, but something&nbsp;
that ended up being an inflection point, whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:21.920 --> 00:28:24.920
you recognized it as such at the time or not?
Chris Beresford-Hill: That's a good question. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.920 --> 00:28:30.067
play golf with my father-in-law, I just learned it&nbsp;
so I'm terrible at it. I don't know if you play?&nbsp;

00:28:30.067 --> 00:28:32.760
Tim Ferriss: Terribly.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah, but it's fun. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:32.760 --> 00:28:37.560
you're on a golf course in the morning, life's not&nbsp;
bad. But I was taught that when you putt, if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:45.320
hole is 20 feet away, what you do is you measure&nbsp;
10 inches in front of the ball on the path to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:45.320 --> 00:28:49.360
hole. So you don't aim for the hole, you aim for&nbsp;
that spot 10 inches in front. And I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:49.360 --> 00:28:56.720
advice landed well with me because I think I kind&nbsp;
of had a sense of my dreams, but I was only really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:56.720 --> 00:29:02.600
looking at the incremental of, "Okay, I made a&nbsp;
Mark Cuban Dallas Mavericks ad, now what I really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.600 --> 00:29:08.387
want is to write something that's going to be on&nbsp;
the back cover of GQ." And that was everything.&nbsp;

00:29:08.387 --> 00:29:10.320
Tim Ferriss: So you had a clear idea though?
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah, and it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:12.734
incremental. I knew that I was not going to —
Tim Ferriss: That was the hole?&nbsp;

00:29:12.734 --> 00:29:16.320
Chris Beresford-Hill: — do the Super Bowl&nbsp;
in my first year, I accepted that. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:16.320 --> 00:29:21.360
every time it was, "Okay, now I'm regularly&nbsp;
writing print ads and print headlines. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:21.360 --> 00:29:26.520
I really want to do a TV production. I really want&nbsp;
to go learn that and do that." And that's bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:26.520 --> 00:29:32.400
and that gave you more weight. And as I did that,&nbsp;
I slowly accrued a body of work. And that's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:32.400 --> 00:29:37.320
beauty of this industry is you can have a resume&nbsp;
and you can say whatever you want about yourself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:37.320 --> 00:29:40.960
but you actually have a basket of your&nbsp;
ideas and you can be appraised on those.&nbsp;

00:29:40.960 --> 00:29:46.600
So I built up just enough to get a job&nbsp;
in San Francisco at Goodby, Silverstein,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:46.600 --> 00:29:51.360
which I think that was my big break because I&nbsp;
was learning my craft in Boston, but I didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.360 --> 00:29:56.480
realize that when I went to Goodby, that was then&nbsp;
and now is still one of the greatest creative ad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:56.480 --> 00:30:02.080
agencies in the world that came up with "Got&nbsp;
Milk?" for God's sake. So it doesn't get that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:02.080 --> 00:30:07.240
much better than that. But when I got there, what&nbsp;
I realized is I had learned how to write in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:07.240 --> 00:30:12.120
Modernista style, and that taught me a great&nbsp;
lesson, which is that it's not totally about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:12.120 --> 00:30:16.800
you. As an advertising writer, you've got to be a&nbsp;
chameleon, you've got to understand not everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:16.800 --> 00:30:22.040
can be your voice, it has to be the correct voice.
Tim Ferriss: I want to come back to San Francisco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:22.040 --> 00:30:25.480
so bookmark for a second. But I've made a&nbsp;
promise earlier that I've not forgotten,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:25.480 --> 00:30:30.640
which is coming back to taste. And I think this&nbsp;
may tie into the Modernista style. So could you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:30.640 --> 00:30:37.760
say a bit more about what characterized the&nbsp;
Modernista style, and just a little bit more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:37.760 --> 00:30:45.160
about being introduced to good taste? Because&nbsp;
there seems to be, this is an overstatement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:45.160 --> 00:30:50.040
but a religious divide among creatives where&nbsp;
on one side you have people who think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:50.040 --> 00:30:54.840
taste can be taught or cultivated, and on the&nbsp;
other side it's like that is nature and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.840 --> 00:31:00.000
nurture, and you either have good taste or you do&nbsp;
not. Now, that might be two people with different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:03.360
definitions of God debating the existence&nbsp;
of God, which is going to go nowhere because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:06.800
they're talking about different things. So could&nbsp;
you tell us a little bit more about Modernista&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:06.800 --> 00:31:12.480
style? What is that? And then good taste.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Well, the first thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:12.480 --> 00:31:17.680
I'll say about it too is I don't know the&nbsp;
nature/nurture answer, but I have personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:17.680 --> 00:31:23.040
thoughts. But I think Lance, who's still in my&nbsp;
life now, by the way, he was a great first boss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:23.040 --> 00:31:30.760
but someone I still talk to regularly, he had a&nbsp;
good way of saying it. He's very self-effacing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:30.760 --> 00:31:35.880
like many really talented — the most talented&nbsp;
person is usually the person that's least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:35.880 --> 00:31:39.800
boastful and least sure of anything.&nbsp;
I've definitely learned that over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:39.800 --> 00:31:45.880
and also radically open-minded versus fixed.
And Lance would say that the Modernista style&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:45.880 --> 00:31:51.440
was kind of you put an idea out there and the vibe&nbsp;
of it is like, "That's cool." They really did know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:51.440 --> 00:31:55.880
how to do one of the hardest things, which is&nbsp;
cool is or isn't, and it's hard to convince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:55.880 --> 00:32:03.120
of cool. But there was just a way of writing,&nbsp;
there's an ad for the big H2 Hummer truck at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:03.120 --> 00:32:08.640
three-quarter angle that made it look almost like&nbsp;
a piece of jewelry. And then the line on it said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:08.640 --> 00:32:14.440
"Perfect for rugby moms." But it was smart,&nbsp;
but it was just kind of bad-ass, kind of sexy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:14.440 --> 00:32:17.680
kind of those things. And so that —
Tim Ferriss: It was succinct also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:18.240 --> 00:32:19.840
very, very succinct.
Chris Beresford-Hill: It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:19.840 --> 00:32:24.040
was very hard to copy. It was very tight&nbsp;
writing and it was beautiful art direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:24.040 --> 00:32:29.320
and that was the hallmark and that was the box&nbsp;
there. And if you came with something that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:29.320 --> 00:32:37.760
too wacky or too dry or too intellectual, too much&nbsp;
maybe like Modernista or too much like a big broad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:37.760 --> 00:32:42.440
Super Bowl ad, it just didn't fit into the kind of&nbsp;
clients that were attracted to Modernista and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:42.440 --> 00:32:49.120
Modernista sort of serves very well. So I really&nbsp;
learned how to find that voice and write in it.&nbsp;

00:32:49.120 --> 00:32:56.000
And then when I went to Goodby, the four ends&nbsp;
of the box just fell down and I realized, okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:56.000 --> 00:33:00.600
you could go to Jeff with something that makes&nbsp;
someone laugh out loud or you could go to him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:00.600 --> 00:33:07.080
with something profound and the color palette&nbsp;
there because of who Jeff and Rich are because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:07.080 --> 00:33:12.600
of the scale and size of the agency and the&nbsp;
amount of talent they'd had through the halls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:12.600 --> 00:33:18.480
over the years, the aperture was just wide.&nbsp;
But at that point, I think some level of taste,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:18.480 --> 00:33:23.720
some level of maybe standards came of it has&nbsp;
to feel fresh, it has to feel interesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:23.720 --> 00:33:32.280
it has to be succinct. Those kind of lessons were&nbsp;
learned and then you could apply them to goofy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:32.280 --> 00:33:41.904
things and profound things. But I think it's&nbsp;
the taste and the skills kind of were merged.&nbsp;

00:33:41.904 --> 00:33:43.960
Tim Ferriss: So part of the reason that&nbsp;
I wanted to have this conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:43.960 --> 00:33:50.160
and we were talking before we started recording or&nbsp;
you asked me rather, "What would you be interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:50.160 --> 00:33:54.520
in or what do you think an audience be interested&nbsp;
in hearing?" And we discussed a few past podcast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:54.520 --> 00:34:04.400
guests who are very iconoclastic and have sort&nbsp;
of painted a unique path for themselves with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:04.400 --> 00:34:10.800
seemingly very few constraints, like Rick Rubin&nbsp;
would come to mind, one of a kind. And part of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:10.800 --> 00:34:16.760
answer was creativity within constraints. Because&nbsp;
the reality for most people, and the reality for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:16.760 --> 00:34:23.920
me more than a lot of people would assume, is that&nbsp;
I'm operating with a team or with contractors or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:23.920 --> 00:34:31.560
with deadlines, I have constraints. I think a lot&nbsp;
of good creativity flourishes with constraints.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:31.560 --> 00:34:36.240
But when you have a team, when you have a boss,&nbsp;
when you have clients, you have to navigate a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:36.240 --> 00:34:42.400
whole host of different hurdles and challenges&nbsp;
than if you're a solo operator trying to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:42.400 --> 00:34:47.960
creator on say, Instagram or TikTok, which has&nbsp;
its own challenges, but they're very different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:47.960 --> 00:34:51.680
And I thought that would be fun to explore.
So one of my questions, as someone who doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:51.680 --> 00:34:56.880
know the business at all really or very, very&nbsp;
little, as you're building the portfolio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:57.840 --> 00:35:03.360
you could have a portfolio that doesn't&nbsp;
pull its weight after a few years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:03.360 --> 00:35:06.920
right? There's the possibility you assemble&nbsp;
a portfolio that doesn't get you to the back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:06.920 --> 00:35:12.680
cover of GQ, that doesn't get you to the&nbsp;
Super Bowl ads. How did you think about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:12.680 --> 00:35:18.400
and I'm not sure how much agency you have with&nbsp;
this, I guess pun intended, building a portfolio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:18.400 --> 00:35:22.080
that would get you closer to the hole? It's like,&nbsp;
okay, you can measure 20 inches out for that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:22.080 --> 00:35:29.640
putt or whatever it might be, how did you think&nbsp;
about portfolio construction and making progress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:29.640 --> 00:35:32.920
towards bigger and more interesting things?
Chris Beresford-Hill: Well, I mean, I suppose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:32.920 --> 00:35:38.960
it's what you're attracted to and what you love.&nbsp;
And I'm not here to say that all of advertising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:38.960 --> 00:35:43.720
when you drive past a billboard or&nbsp;
flick on Instagram or turn on a TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:43.720 --> 00:35:48.160
it's mostly not good. And in fact, most of&nbsp;
it is not even ideas, I think oftentimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:48.160 --> 00:35:55.440
it's just information. So there's a lot of it.
And I think for whatever reason my interest in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:55.440 --> 00:36:01.120
the industry latched on to somewhere in the top 20&nbsp;
percent of the — because I don't think I wanted a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:01.120 --> 00:36:05.280
job in advertising, I think I wanted to express&nbsp;
myself in an interesting way and I wanted to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:05.280 --> 00:36:11.960
provocative or interesting ideas into the world.&nbsp;
So I think I was obsessed with the best of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:11.960 --> 00:36:16.600
best. And so I would, in my early years, spend&nbsp;
time with award show annuals and look at what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:16.600 --> 00:36:20.160
industry said were the best. And I'd form my own&nbsp;
opinions and I would say, "Okay, I think that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:20.160 --> 00:36:26.200
okay, but I love that." So I started to shape&nbsp;
where I wanted to go with it. And then of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:26.200 --> 00:36:30.240
you figure out, "Okay, well what are the places&nbsp;
or who are the people that are doing that, and how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:30.240 --> 00:36:34.120
the hell do I get near them?" And that became —
Tim Ferriss: And are those people who are on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:34.120 --> 00:36:39.800
creative side? Are those clients? Are those both?&nbsp;
How much control do you have over what you do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:39.800 --> 00:36:44.600
next, right? One thing you said that surprised me&nbsp;
was you came up with an idea for a client and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:44.600 --> 00:36:51.240
sort of pitched it to a client who is not already&nbsp;
signed with the agency. So I was like, "Oh, okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:51.240 --> 00:36:55.120
that's interesting," because I would assume in&nbsp;
a big agency it's like, "We have these clients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:55.120 --> 00:37:01.360
and here's your assignment." So I don't know how&nbsp;
much control you have in sort of forging your own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:01.360 --> 00:37:05.480
destiny with new clients, being around new people.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Well, like anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:05.480 --> 00:37:09.040
I think it's like if you are being challenged and&nbsp;
if you have all the opportunities under the sun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:09.040 --> 00:37:15.400
on your current roster or whatever your workload&nbsp;
is, then you give your all to that. But if you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:15.400 --> 00:37:20.360
feeling like maybe there's a lower ceiling on some&nbsp;
of the things in front of you, then naturally I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:20.360 --> 00:37:24.840
think you've just got to get proactive. Because,&nbsp;
again, at the end of the day when the clock runs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:24.840 --> 00:37:30.040
out or when the year is over, whatever you made is&nbsp;
what you have to show for it. So if things start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:30.040 --> 00:37:35.680
to go south, I mean, sometimes there'll be maybe&nbsp;
a host of great clients, but maybe the CEO has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:35.680 --> 00:37:40.120
very questionable taste, doesn't like ideas that&nbsp;
are too big or bold or creative. And you've got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:40.120 --> 00:37:43.760
account for that and you've got to kind of hedge&nbsp;
on that, you've got to have a lot of irons in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:43.760 --> 00:37:48.320
fire. And when you're feeling like things are&nbsp;
stagnating or going south, you've got to almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:48.320 --> 00:37:52.200
invent your own opportunities. So it's a mix.
Tim Ferriss: How did you get your first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:52.200 --> 00:37:54.520
Super Bowl ad? How did that happen?
Chris Beresford-Hill: It's a little bit of — it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:54.520 --> 00:38:01.200
may be a nature/nurture thing as well. The&nbsp;
nurture is my dad who kind of figured out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:01.200 --> 00:38:07.720
how to remove obstacles to where he was going. To&nbsp;
get a Super Bowl brief is and was a very big deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:07.720 --> 00:38:13.840
but I think maybe 10, 15 years ago things were&nbsp;
much more hierarchical. I think today it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:13.840 --> 00:38:20.400
uncommon to give big opportunities&nbsp;
to all levels of creative people or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:20.400 --> 00:38:23.960
any strategist or account people, anyone&nbsp;
in the agency. But back then it was very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:23.960 --> 00:38:30.640
hierarchical. And again, this is the most&nbsp;
1A thing, but I went and I asked for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:30.640 --> 00:38:31.920
assignment and I had a friend —
Tim Ferriss: Can you place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:31.920 --> 00:38:33.760
us in time? Where are you?
Chris Beresford-Hill: So this would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:33.760 --> 00:38:42.160
be 2005 in San Francisco at Goodby, Silverstein,&nbsp;
and Partners. And the previous two years they'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:42.160 --> 00:38:47.360
done a Super Bowl ad for Emerald Nuts. And so I&nbsp;
went to the creative director of it and I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:47.360 --> 00:38:51.800
"I very much love this campaign. It's one of&nbsp;
the reasons why I was so excited to be here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:51.800 --> 00:38:56.600
If you would have me, it doesn't even have&nbsp;
to be formally assigned. I would just give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:56.600 --> 00:39:00.858
anything to work on it." And that was it.
Tim Ferriss: That's a smart way to word it.&nbsp;

00:39:00.858 --> 00:39:02.507
Chris Beresford-Hill: Make it an&nbsp;
easy yes. If you make yourself.&nbsp;

00:39:02.507 --> 00:39:04.920
Tim Ferriss: Doesn't have to be&nbsp;
formally assigned. I mean, that probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:04.920 --> 00:39:11.560
simplifies the political/approvals dynamic.
Chris Beresford-Hill: That's right. And it gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:11.560 --> 00:39:19.720
that creative lead. I was scoped for two teams,&nbsp;
I just found a third writer. So I did that and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:19.720 --> 00:39:23.560
was fortunate, it was a guy named Steve Simpson&nbsp;
who worked there, it was him and Jeff Goodby&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:23.560 --> 00:39:33.400
together. And we got the brief, and the brief was,&nbsp;
"Win the Super Bowl ad meter," and that means win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:33.400 --> 00:39:41.680
the popularity contest. So peanuts are parody, so&nbsp;
just do our peanut ad and make it amazing. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:41.680 --> 00:39:48.400
remember we were sitting in a conference room on&nbsp;
the sixth floor and everyone kind of funneled out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:48.400 --> 00:39:55.160
and I felt like I was so close to this chance to&nbsp;
do a Super Bowl ad that I needed it, I had to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:55.160 --> 00:40:00.240
myself the best shot to win. So the strategist was&nbsp;
one of the last people to leave the room. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:00.240 --> 00:40:05.400
strategists are the ones that work with the client&nbsp;
and they align on what needs to be communicated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:05.400 --> 00:40:09.267
what are the core elements and what do we know&nbsp;
about our audience and that kind of stuff.&nbsp;

00:40:09.267 --> 00:40:12.320
Tim Ferriss: So this is not to diminish the&nbsp;
role, but it's kind of like an account manager,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:18.320
they work with that specific client to&nbsp;
translate their needs and preferences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:18.320 --> 00:40:21.800
and so on to the in-house creative folks?
Chris Beresford-Hill: That's right. To kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:21.800 --> 00:40:26.360
of translate the ask from the client into&nbsp;
something that might be inspiring in a brief&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:26.360 --> 00:40:30.680
that would get the creatives thinking. And it&nbsp;
was a guy named Matt Herman, and I remember I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:30.680 --> 00:40:34.000
kind of grabbed him and he was a nice guy and&nbsp;
I knew him socially. And I just said, "Hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:34.000 --> 00:40:39.200
just tell me, is there anything that I could put&nbsp;
in this that would make the client like it even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:39.200 --> 00:40:46.640
more?" And he had said, "Well, we're having these&nbsp;
conversations about maybe thinking about peanuts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:46.640 --> 00:40:52.440
as a source of energy, not just like a snack,&nbsp;
but something that can give you a pick me up."&nbsp;

00:40:52.440 --> 00:40:59.720
And so I took that and then I ran and I wrote&nbsp;
20 scripts, but every single one of them had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:59.720 --> 00:41:03.560
the peanuts as this source of energy that gave&nbsp;
you something, that got you out of something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:03.560 --> 00:41:08.560
that protected you from something, made them&nbsp;
feel like more of a utility. And as a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:08.560 --> 00:41:14.080
I kind of gamed the system. And then all the ads I&nbsp;
wrote were the ones that went forward because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:14.080 --> 00:41:21.440
did more. And table stakes are table stakes, in&nbsp;
anything, in creativity or whatever your job is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:21.440 --> 00:41:26.800
if you can figure out one more thing that&nbsp;
what you're being asked to do could do and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:26.800 --> 00:41:32.387
you cover everything and that, you're more&nbsp;
attractive, you're more appealing always.&nbsp;

00:41:32.387 --> 00:41:36.440
Tim Ferriss: And I want to highlight also that&nbsp;
first you have to have the chops, you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:36.440 --> 00:41:44.600
have the ability, the skillset to execute. But so&nbsp;
much is left on the table by not asking targeted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:44.600 --> 00:41:51.000
questions, right? You asked the question,&nbsp;
which was the spell that provided you with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:51.000 --> 00:41:56.440
the information you needed to use the hook that&nbsp;
the client would respond to. All right, so then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:56.440 --> 00:42:03.440
what happens? What does the approval process or&nbsp;
production process and brief look like? All right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:03.440 --> 00:42:10.114
so you send off these 20 different scripts for,&nbsp;
what is it, I don't know how long the spot is?&nbsp;

00:42:10.114 --> 00:42:10.760
Chris Beresford-Hill: 30 seconds.
Tim Ferriss: 30 seconds?&nbsp;

00:42:10.760 --> 00:42:15.280
Chris Beresford-Hill: So it fits in a page. The&nbsp;
average 30-second spot is like three-quarters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:15.280 --> 00:42:17.120
of a page in a script.
Tim Ferriss: How much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:17.120 --> 00:42:21.240
does a Super Bowl ad cost these days?
Chris Beresford-Hill: These days? Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:21.240 --> 00:42:25.627
boy, oh, boy. I want to say a&nbsp;
30 is like six or seven million.&nbsp;

00:42:25.627 --> 00:42:27.960
Tim Ferriss: Six or seven million?
Chris Beresford-Hill: I think a 60 is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:27.960 --> 00:42:34.080
somewhere in the 10 to 12. At this point, I think&nbsp;
Super Bowl was the Super Bowl, but I think it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:34.080 --> 00:42:38.120
like $1 million in 2005, 2006.
Tim Ferriss: Non-trivial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:38.120 --> 00:42:39.507
though. I mean, this is a big bet.
Chris Beresford-Hill: It was a big bet.&nbsp;

00:42:39.507 --> 00:42:44.160
Tim Ferriss: Also reputationally a big deal for&nbsp;
people within the client company. All right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:44.160 --> 00:42:50.240
so 30-second spot, obviously a huge&nbsp;
investment for the company, if not in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:50.240 --> 00:42:53.667
capital, certainly in reputation.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Sure.&nbsp;

00:42:53.667 --> 00:42:56.000
Tim Ferriss: What happens?
Chris Beresford-Hill: Well, so I wrote a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:56.000 --> 00:43:04.640
them, and we can chat a little bit later about the&nbsp;
techniques, but I spilled my brains and I wrote —&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:04.640 --> 00:43:09.000
the trick when you're coming up with creative&nbsp;
ideas, I think everyone has slightly different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:09.000 --> 00:43:15.320
techniques, but I think the one consistent thing&nbsp;
is you have to cajole yourself to release every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:15.320 --> 00:43:21.560
possible thought you can and get it all out of&nbsp;
your head. And then when you think you're empty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:21.560 --> 00:43:26.800
you have to trick yourself into coming up with&nbsp;
more ideas. You've got to mine yourself. You've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:26.800 --> 00:43:32.640
got to say, "What is the ad I think we should&nbsp;
make? What is the absolute wrong ad to make? What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:32.640 --> 00:43:39.720
is the worst ad to make? What is the ad my hero&nbsp;
David Fincher would make? What is the ad Peter and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:39.720 --> 00:43:46.040
Bobby Farrelly would make?" My friend Jason has&nbsp;
one where he says, "What would I do if the laws of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:46.040 --> 00:43:53.800
gravity and physics were not in place?" You have&nbsp;
to fuck with yourself. And it's almost like trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:53.800 --> 00:44:00.200
to make yourself vomit basically. I've never said&nbsp;
it that way before, but you got to get it all out.&nbsp;

00:44:00.200 --> 00:44:04.240
And then essentially when you're a babe, when&nbsp;
you're young, you bring all that raw material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:04.240 --> 00:44:07.600
and a creative director tells you what's good,&nbsp;
That's how you learn some tastes. A good creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:07.600 --> 00:44:10.720
director says, "These three are the interesting&nbsp;
ones." As you get better, you start to be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:10.720 --> 00:44:15.920
sift your own material, but you've got to get all&nbsp;
the material out. Now, 20-plus years in, I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:15.920 --> 00:44:22.320
I can vomit in my head and extract the bits, but I&nbsp;
think that's the journey is get everything inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:22.320 --> 00:44:27.360
of you out and then in the beginning someone&nbsp;
tells you what it is. In the middle, you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:27.360 --> 00:44:32.080
it out and then you work through it yourself. And&nbsp;
then in the end you can have your private vomits.&nbsp;

00:44:32.640 --> 00:44:37.480
But anyway, so I wrote all kinds of them, and the&nbsp;
one that went through, I used it, I trapped myself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:37.480 --> 00:44:44.320
to write it. And a few years ago I was reading&nbsp;
something or I saw an interview with the artist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:44.320 --> 00:44:50.440
Kaws, K-A-W-S, I'm sure you have seen his stuff.&nbsp;
He does those big kind of vinyl Disney-looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:50.440 --> 00:44:52.600
characters with the Xs for eyes.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I'd have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:52.600 --> 00:44:55.440
check it out. Yeah, all right, got it.
Chris Beresford-Hill: It's really good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:55.440 --> 00:45:04.320
pop art and he sometimes uses these crazy color&nbsp;
combinations. And someone asked him how he put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:04.320 --> 00:45:09.360
together such crazy combinations of colors. And&nbsp;
what he would say is he kind of reverse-painted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:09.360 --> 00:45:13.720
himself into a corner. He would start in a corner&nbsp;
with a bunch of colors that didn't make sense, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:13.720 --> 00:45:18.160
then he used the rest of the canvas to make sense&nbsp;
of it. So he forced himself to make sense out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:18.160 --> 00:45:23.560
of what he did here all the way here. And that I&nbsp;
learned later but that was just something I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:23.560 --> 00:45:28.920
do to myself. So I started writing a sentence&nbsp;
and I didn't know what the end of the sentence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:28.920 --> 00:45:33.640
would be. And I would do that to myself a lot just&nbsp;
to force myself. I would start an answer without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:33.640 --> 00:45:37.658
knowing what the answer was, and then I would —
Tim Ferriss: How would you pick the beginning?&nbsp;

00:45:37.658 --> 00:45:41.040
Chris Beresford-Hill: I would start logically.&nbsp;
So I thought about that, the brief I got to with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:41.040 --> 00:45:47.680
my friend Matt was an afternoon energy slump and&nbsp;
peanuts are a good pick me up. So I wrote around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:47.680 --> 00:45:54.600
3:00 p.m. when your blood sugar and energy are&nbsp;
low, and then I sat there and then I wrote "Robert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:54.600 --> 00:46:02.640
Goulet appears and messes with your things," but&nbsp;
I didn't have that idea, I just started a sentence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:02.640 --> 00:46:08.440
and I said, "Finish it, fucker." And so that was,&nbsp;
again, one of a bunch of ideas. But that was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:08.440 --> 00:46:17.720
idea was basically to turn Robert Goulet, the now&nbsp;
passed, but the iconic crooner and singer of "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:17.720 --> 00:46:22.720
Impossible Dream," to turn him into an afternoon&nbsp;
poltergeist. And so basically, I think I had also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:22.720 --> 00:46:29.520
seen that incredible Fatboy Slim Spike Jonze video&nbsp;
with Chris Walken. I think many of us saw that and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:29.520 --> 00:46:33.680
went, "Oh, my God, I want something like that."So&nbsp;
I had the sentence I wrote, and then I thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:33.680 --> 00:46:38.920
"Let's make this a weird kind of dance around an&nbsp;
office where everyone's asleep and Robert Goulet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:38.920 --> 00:46:43.440
is pouring coffee on keyboards and taping&nbsp;
people to chairs and just doing whatever."&nbsp;

00:46:43.440 --> 00:46:51.240
So anyway, so it's written, and I think&nbsp;
another thing obviously is I show my bosses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:51.240 --> 00:46:56.040
and it's Steve and it's Jeff Goodby, and Jeff&nbsp;
Goodby is in the advertising world, one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:56.040 --> 00:47:02.960
Mount Rushmore kind of figures. So I also learned&nbsp;
if he's 10 times better than me, don't try to sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:02.960 --> 00:47:08.938
him on anything he knows. So that's how I read it.
Tim Ferriss: Meaning let him judge for himself.&nbsp;

00:47:08.938 --> 00:47:11.280
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah, okay, "This one's&nbsp;
crazy. Oh, you're going to love this." No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:11.280 --> 00:47:13.680
I just said, "Okay, here it is."
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:13.680 --> 00:47:16.560
pro tip, don't ever say that to —
Chris Beresford-Hill: No, it's true.&nbsp;

00:47:16.560 --> 00:47:19.587
Tim Ferriss: "You're going to love this."
Chris Beresford-Hill: No, don't do that.&nbsp;

00:47:19.587 --> 00:47:22.218
Tim Ferriss: Don't laugh too hard at&nbsp;
your own jokes at the time either.&nbsp;

00:47:22.218 --> 00:47:25.880
Chris Beresford-Hill: So they liked it. I&nbsp;
think they liked it and they're like, "Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:25.880 --> 00:47:34.840
that's cool." And then what ended up happening&nbsp;
again is the craziest ad ideas are really highly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:34.840 --> 00:47:40.920
highly rational strategies brought to life in&nbsp;
highly, highly surprising, memorable ways. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:40.920 --> 00:47:45.040
when we went to the client with it, and it was a&nbsp;
guy named Andrew Burke, who was the CMO of Emerald&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:45.040 --> 00:47:50.520
Nuts at the time, we had a very literal idea.&nbsp;
We had an idea about a little Mick from Rocky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:50.520 --> 00:47:55.760
kind of coach that would keep you awake in the&nbsp;
afternoon and tell you to eat. We had stuff that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:55.760 --> 00:48:03.960
was a little bit on the nose. But to his credit,&nbsp;
he didn't see this weird poltergeist thing. He's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:03.960 --> 00:48:09.200
like, "Oh, I get it. We're going to show people&nbsp;
that there's actual danger in being sluggish in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:09.200 --> 00:48:13.320
the afternoon and we're going to make up what&nbsp;
that is and we'll position ourselves." So he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:13.320 --> 00:48:19.680
immediately saw past the kind of insanity of the&nbsp;
execution to the strategy. And ultimately a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:19.680 --> 00:48:24.840
client does that and great work does that.
And I was talking to my friends Eric and Craig,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:24.840 --> 00:48:32.760
who did the Old Spice, "The man your man could&nbsp;
smell like," and that is also a completely batshit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:32.760 --> 00:48:40.360
idea. But the strategy was, "We make body wash for&nbsp;
guys, but their lady friends buy it for them." And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:40.360 --> 00:48:45.760
they said, "Great, we're just going to have a hot&nbsp;
guy talk to ladies about it." And again, and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:45.760 --> 00:48:49.938
they made it entertaining but it's almost like —
Tim Ferriss: There's a rationale behind it?&nbsp;

00:48:49.938 --> 00:48:53.800
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah, and it's simple&nbsp;
and it's bone simple. And then the creativity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:53.800 --> 00:48:58.880
and the surprising connections you make and the&nbsp;
surprising things you write, that's the magic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:58.880 --> 00:49:04.119
But it's always magic on top of something very&nbsp;
simple and easy to understand, it has to be. So to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:04.119 --> 00:49:10.000
go back to this, so we go through it. So this idea&nbsp;
is sold, and I will say, to this client's credit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:11.120 --> 00:49:16.120
it was not over-researched or heavily researched.&nbsp;
So what they did is they put it into some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:16.120 --> 00:49:22.200
quantitative research to show the storyboards. We&nbsp;
made some storyboards and a moderator shared it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:22.200 --> 00:49:25.600
with a panel of people, and it was not —
Tim Ferriss: Panel meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:25.600 --> 00:49:28.640
almost like a focus group?
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah, they were like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:28.640 --> 00:49:33.080
people roughly in the demographic. And they were&nbsp;
kind of read the script. It wasn't performed, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:33.080 --> 00:49:39.000
was read by a third party. And instead of saying&nbsp;
the dreaded, "Do you like it?" Which is how many a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:39.000 --> 00:49:45.640
great idea gets unraveled, they just said, "Okay,&nbsp;
so what did you see? What do you understand from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:45.640 --> 00:49:51.720
it? What did it make you understand about peanuts?&nbsp;
And what do you think of Emerald Nuts now?" Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:51.720 --> 00:49:58.080
it was asking customers the right questions. I&nbsp;
think sometimes the Henry Ford, "Ask people what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:58.080 --> 00:50:03.080
they want and they'll say 'A faster horse,'" I&nbsp;
think sometimes when you ask your audience to tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:03.080 --> 00:50:08.920
you what it should be, you get a lot of mirroring.&nbsp;
But when you make the conversation about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:08.920 --> 00:50:13.640
are you getting from it, then you can give them&nbsp;
something unexpected and you can push yourself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:13.640 --> 00:50:16.520
So we did that kind of research, thank God.
Tim Ferriss: Those are good questions. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:16.520 --> 00:50:22.320
makes me think of something that is maybe only&nbsp;
tangentially related, but something Kevin Kelly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:22.320 --> 00:50:28.440
who's a friend of mine who's been on the podcast&nbsp;
who's very wise in a lot of ways, and he has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:28.440 --> 00:50:33.800
book of advice, short, pithy lines that started&nbsp;
as a list of lessons learned on his, I believe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:33.800 --> 00:50:37.867
68th birthday to give to his kids.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Cool.&nbsp;

00:50:37.867 --> 00:50:41.000
Tim Ferriss: And one of them was, "If&nbsp;
people tell you something is wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:41.000 --> 00:50:44.000
they're almost always right. When they&nbsp;
tell you how to fix it, they're almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:44.000 --> 00:50:51.720
always wrong." But people have a gut sense, so&nbsp;
these questions pair well with that insight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:51.720 --> 00:50:56.000
I think. Okay, so those questions are asked?
Chris Beresford-Hill: Those questions are asked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:56.000 --> 00:51:01.120
and it passes. People say what we wanted them to&nbsp;
say. And of course, again, maybe the reason why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:01.120 --> 00:51:06.480
this one sailed through it, I have many a horror&nbsp;
story was, again, because they got a natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:06.480 --> 00:51:11.200
energy message when they had convinced their&nbsp;
board they were just going to entertain. So it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:11.200 --> 00:51:17.560
already in the bonus round, so it was favored. And&nbsp;
that gives you that lift so you can move through.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:17.560 --> 00:51:22.577
So then we hired a director. It was a duo called&nbsp;
The Perlorian Brothers out of Toronto, Canada.&nbsp;

00:51:22.577 --> 00:51:23.640
Tim Ferriss: That's an amazing name.
Chris Beresford-Hill: I don't know where, Ian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:23.640 --> 00:51:24.320
and Michael —
Tim Ferriss: Sounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:24.320 --> 00:51:28.480
like an acrobatic act from Cirque du Soleil.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah, I know, I know. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:28.480 --> 00:51:35.760
they're these two former ad creatives that became&nbsp;
directors and they got into it and we did a call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:35.760 --> 00:51:43.160
with Robert and his wife Vera. And I thought we&nbsp;
can talk a little bit about celebrity advertising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:43.160 --> 00:51:48.560
because this was my greatest experience because&nbsp;
normally celebrities are very specific on what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:48.560 --> 00:51:54.040
they will or won't do, and there's always some&nbsp;
pushes from their team. But Robert just wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:54.040 --> 00:51:58.160
to know if he could choose his own wardrobe. And&nbsp;
we said, "Absolutely, sounds good." So we filmed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:58.160 --> 00:52:07.280
it in Toronto over two days, and it was very fun.
Again, and this is probably maybe inside baseball&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:07.280 --> 00:52:14.200
and the industry, but I think sometimes clients&nbsp;
become so creative that they mirror the job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:14.200 --> 00:52:18.960
but sometimes the best clients are let the&nbsp;
creatives be creative and you're there to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:19.520 --> 00:52:24.280
"I think this is okay," "I'm not comfortable with&nbsp;
this," "Here's a note on why making this change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:24.280 --> 00:52:28.160
would actually be more effective, not less." But&nbsp;
our client, Andrew, was just there to supervise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:28.160 --> 00:52:32.880
and make sure that we were getting the right&nbsp;
stuff in the can, protecting his investment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:32.880 --> 00:52:37.560
but giving us liberal room to play. So it was&nbsp;
a magical shoot, it was right before Christmas.&nbsp;

00:52:37.560 --> 00:52:43.400
And then you hire an editor separately. And&nbsp;
so there's an editor named Ian McKenzie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:43.400 --> 00:52:48.120
who's cut a ton of Super Bowl ads and he's&nbsp;
very good at it. And early January we go into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:48.120 --> 00:52:56.160
his suite in the Flatiron in New York City, and&nbsp;
I'm super excited, and he plays it and I'm super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:56.160 --> 00:53:01.200
disappointed because it didn't live up to all&nbsp;
my dreams. I don't know, it feels flat. Is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:01.920 --> 00:53:08.720
funny? I could see nothing but the problems with&nbsp;
it. And what you end up doing with the editors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:08.720 --> 00:53:13.640
I think back in the day when you had to cut film&nbsp;
with a razor blade and use tape, you would debate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:13.640 --> 00:53:19.200
and edit. But because you're doing things&nbsp;
at the time final cut, you can try anything.&nbsp;

00:53:19.200 --> 00:53:22.000
Tim Ferriss: You can ready, fire, aim.
Chris Beresford-Hill: You can make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:22.000 --> 00:53:25.560
backwards. In some ways, it allows you to explore&nbsp;
anything, but maybe in other ways it takes some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:25.560 --> 00:53:30.080
the thinking out of it, which could be a shame.&nbsp;
But anyway, we go through a couple of days where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:30.080 --> 00:53:34.600
we try a whole bunch of different things, we&nbsp;
try some different music, we change the order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:34.600 --> 00:53:40.480
of this little afternoon maraud, we experiment.&nbsp;
And then what I've learned with Ian, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:40.480 --> 00:53:46.320
I've now been working with him for 15 years,&nbsp;
ultimately we start to feel really good about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:46.320 --> 00:53:49.400
me and the art director I was working with.
Tim Ferriss: How much of that was the product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:49.400 --> 00:53:54.240
changing versus your psychology adapting to&nbsp;
the fact that perhaps nothing could live up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:54.240 --> 00:53:59.760
to the swirling dream of perfection in your mind?
Chris Beresford-Hill: It's the latter, it's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:59.760 --> 00:54:04.880
latter. Yeah, you're exactly right. And&nbsp;
what I've learned with Ian, and I just,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:04.880 --> 00:54:08.960
for a Super Bowl ad we made this year, I brought&nbsp;
a young team for the first time to work with him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:08.960 --> 00:54:15.440
so I have fun, I'm seeing them get to do what I&nbsp;
did 15 years ago. And the advice I gave them is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:15.440 --> 00:54:21.480
I said, "Just know whatever Ian shows you&nbsp;
first is going to be closest to where you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:21.480 --> 00:54:25.560
end," because that's what I know about him. So we&nbsp;
kind of walked around the moon with him, and of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:25.560 --> 00:54:30.400
course it was like maybe some music tweaks, maybe&nbsp;
a little bit of tightening up a few moments here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:30.400 --> 00:54:36.360
but ultimately he had it. But we had to get our&nbsp;
brains to catch up with reality. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:36.960 --> 00:54:40.200
at the end of the day when someone has&nbsp;
never seen it, they're going to kind of see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:40.200 --> 00:54:46.080
the best version of it. But you torture it.
Tim Ferriss: Editors are magicians. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:46.080 --> 00:54:55.960
the good editors in any medium are so important,&nbsp;
yet so frequently semi-invisible, right? And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:55.960 --> 00:55:02.680
think I am getting this roughly right enough, but&nbsp;
I believe if you look at Steven Spielberg's films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:02.680 --> 00:55:10.600
and his greatest hits, the greatest constant, I&nbsp;
believe, is his film editor, yet how many people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:10.600 --> 00:55:17.320
could name his name? I wish I could name his name.&nbsp;
I've read the story, I've researched this guy, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:17.320 --> 00:55:23.840
he is one of the masters at work again and again.
Chris Beresford-Hill: More happens in the edit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:23.840 --> 00:55:30.040
than people think because it's not lining&nbsp;
it up in order, it's speeding up shots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:30.040 --> 00:55:35.160
it's arts and crafts, it's punching in. The&nbsp;
amount of raw stuff we do in the edit where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:35.160 --> 00:55:38.600
we'll split the screen, take this, take from&nbsp;
that, and the amount of experimentation that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:38.600 --> 00:55:43.840
happens there. And that's really, in many&nbsp;
ways, that's the most important moment.&nbsp;

00:55:43.840 --> 00:55:51.880
And so we walked with this edit and I showed it to&nbsp;
Jeff Goodby and Steve Simpson, and they liked it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:51.880 --> 00:55:58.680
and it was good. And then a very interesting thing&nbsp;
happened, I had the QuickTime on my computer and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:58.680 --> 00:56:05.000
showed a couple people in the office and I played&nbsp;
it — why would I say their name? But I played it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:05.000 --> 00:56:16.160
for a couple people and they went, "Huh." And I&nbsp;
distinctly remember this. I showed two or three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:16.160 --> 00:56:22.840
people and everyone — and by the way, I think some&nbsp;
people respond as professionals. They immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:22.840 --> 00:56:28.040
think what they would've done with the opportunity&nbsp;
maybe or I don't know what. But for some reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:28.040 --> 00:56:34.480
and I don't know why, it didn't bother me at&nbsp;
all, and in many ways I started getting a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:34.480 --> 00:56:39.480
excited by the fact that people didn't get it.
And I've not really unpacked this, I'm talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:39.480 --> 00:56:43.960
about this kind of long-form for the first time&nbsp;
in a long time but I kind of felt like there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:43.960 --> 00:56:49.320
something more special happening if it wasn't&nbsp;
an easy get for people. And I started to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:49.320 --> 00:56:56.680
that it might be more original. And I think that&nbsp;
was the case and I kind of learned a great lesson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:56.680 --> 00:57:02.280
there, which is once you take feedback and&nbsp;
definitely share your ideas, but once you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:02.280 --> 00:57:07.040
locked in and you're sure you're good with it,&nbsp;
you really don't have to look back. It didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:07.040 --> 00:57:11.400
rattle me, and I'm glad it didn't. And I think to&nbsp;
this day, if there's something that I believe in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:11.400 --> 00:57:15.960
if I get lukewarm results, it just does not phase&nbsp;
me at all. And usually you'll find your audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:15.960 --> 00:57:19.760
people will like it, people will respond&nbsp;
differently than your inner circles.&nbsp;

00:57:19.760 --> 00:57:24.080
Tim Ferriss: What happened when the ad came out?
Chris Beresford-Hill: It did well, it did really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:24.080 --> 00:57:29.480
well. The Ad Age the next morning ranked&nbsp;
it number one, they had an ad critiquer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:29.480 --> 00:57:31.720
named Bob Garfield who —
Tim Ferriss: He was like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:31.720 --> 00:57:33.560
the Siskel and Ebert of ads?
Chris Beresford-Hill: He was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:34.360 --> 00:57:37.600
and I feel like Jeff Goodby him had went&nbsp;
head-to-head a few times. And Jeff wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:37.600 --> 00:57:44.320
a big fan, but he liked it. And I think it was&nbsp;
kind of early days of YouTube, and I think it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:44.880 --> 00:57:49.640
for a day or two, it was the number one comedy&nbsp;
video on YouTube or something crazy like that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:49.640 --> 00:57:52.320
So it worked and the sales went up.
Tim Ferriss: I saw it back then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:52.320 --> 00:57:57.080
I remember seeing it. It was weird because it&nbsp;
got sent to me and it was on YouTube, I recall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:57.080 --> 00:58:06.200
this very specifically. So I want to unpack a&nbsp;
couple of concepts/lessons from a few different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:06.200 --> 00:58:11.640
people and I want you to explain them. And then&nbsp;
we're going to go to lying to the CMO of Adidas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:11.640 --> 00:58:17.360
if that's the right way to put it. That's what&nbsp;
we call a cliffhanger. After this commercial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:17.360 --> 00:58:29.520
break. No commercial break. But the first is from&nbsp;
David Lubars, if I'm getting that right. And I'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:29.520 --> 00:58:36.440
love for you to share what advice or lessons you&nbsp;
took from him. Now one that I have in front of me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:36.440 --> 00:58:40.960
which could be a starting point or maybe is the&nbsp;
point, make as many decisions as possible. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:40.960 --> 00:58:48.480
not clear on what this means, but it does pique&nbsp;
my curiosity. So who is David, and what does this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:48.480 --> 00:58:55.560
mean, and any other lessons learned or principles?
Chris Beresford-Hill: David is the worldwide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:55.560 --> 00:59:01.360
chairman and chief creative officer of&nbsp;
BBDO. I worked for him for eight years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:01.360 --> 00:59:07.200
and I'm not shy of saying he was the best boss I&nbsp;
ever had. He was the best boss I ever had because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:08.360 --> 00:59:13.760
he was very clear, and he was very consistent,&nbsp;
and that's what you need in leadership. So you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:13.760 --> 00:59:19.480
could show him a bunch of work, and he would tell&nbsp;
you what he liked and what he chose right away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:19.480 --> 00:59:26.640
and that was that. He didn't need to split&nbsp;
hairs. He would come give you his response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:26.640 --> 00:59:31.480
and he knew that he would make a bigger impact&nbsp;
in this giant company by going around and saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:31.480 --> 00:59:35.080
"I think this is the most right thing, the most&nbsp;
creative thing, the most interesting thing," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:35.080 --> 00:59:42.000
just go and do that, and not be afraid to make&nbsp;
decisions, create progress. By the way, if he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:42.000 --> 00:59:46.160
picked something that was wrong, well whoop-de-do,&nbsp;
we'd have to go back and do it again. Or maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:46.160 --> 00:59:52.040
we had one that wasn't as good as it could have&nbsp;
been, but he never inhibited progress. He always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:52.040 --> 00:59:55.520
facilitated progress by doing that.
Tim Ferriss: Meaning catalyzing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:55.520 --> 00:59:58.240
some type of forward motion.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Exactly. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:58.240 --> 01:00:02.360
never would say, "Let me take this back, let&nbsp;
me think about it, or let's pull more people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:02.360 --> 01:00:07.640
into it." He would just give you these gut reads,&nbsp;
and it always gave you permission to go forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:07.640 --> 01:00:13.200
and it really taught that someone who's really&nbsp;
pushing everyone and keeping everyone in motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:13.200 --> 01:00:17.120
just happens to always be at the center of so&nbsp;
much. That's why for that eight years, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:17.120 --> 01:00:21.320
the agency was one of the most awarded in the&nbsp;
world. There was my little corner of the universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:21.320 --> 01:00:25.080
but there was all these other corners, and&nbsp;
he just amplified himself by empowering his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:25.080 --> 01:00:30.147
people and saying a lot of yes, sometimes a&nbsp;
no, but he was always game to move forward.&nbsp;

01:00:30.147 --> 01:00:36.400
Tim Ferriss: Were you able to emulate that at&nbsp;
the time in some small way or is that something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:36.400 --> 01:00:40.887
that you were only able to implement later as you&nbsp;
had more and more direct reports or bigger team?&nbsp;

01:00:40.887 --> 01:00:44.520
Chris Beresford-Hill: I used it. I used it&nbsp;
then because — well, because BBO at that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:44.520 --> 01:00:51.160
time was David's place, so David said to do it.&nbsp;
David said, "Let's go," and whether or not — I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:51.160 --> 01:00:54.600
don't think we ever got a look at an org chart&nbsp;
or reporting structure. I don't think we ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:54.600 --> 01:00:59.200
knew that that carried muster, but a lot of&nbsp;
us loved the decisions he was making, and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:59.200 --> 01:01:05.080
enough of us would constantly say, "That's what&nbsp;
David wants." So whether or not that was be all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:05.080 --> 01:01:12.400
end all, it became be all, end all. So we were all&nbsp;
emboldened to make moves, but I myself couldn't.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:12.400 --> 01:01:15.440
There wasn't much I could do in terms of —
Tim Ferriss: In terms of making&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:15.440 --> 01:01:17.039
your own decisions?
Chris Beresford-Hill: No.&nbsp;

01:01:17.039 --> 01:01:17.760
Tim Ferriss: Right.
Chris Beresford-Hill: No. I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:17.760 --> 01:01:23.080
probably working through my own process, but it&nbsp;
was invaluable later because you do see people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:23.080 --> 01:01:29.560
that in a high creative role, you've really&nbsp;
got to make things happen, and you will see&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:29.560 --> 01:01:33.742
some people in very senior roles that really&nbsp;
gild the lily and that really obsess over —&nbsp;

01:01:33.742 --> 01:01:35.227
Tim Ferriss: It's such a great expression.
Chris Beresford-Hill: It is.&nbsp;

01:01:35.227 --> 01:01:36.040
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Chris Beresford-Hill: By the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:36.040 --> 01:01:41.600
the gilding the lily of someone who's looking&nbsp;
at a two-minute case study for an award show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:41.600 --> 01:01:45.760
and changing the mix on the music&nbsp;
eight times, and you're like, "Listen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:45.760 --> 01:01:49.960
it doesn't really matter." There's times&nbsp;
to craft the hell out of it, but there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:49.960 --> 01:01:56.520
also this step back. I think in our industry&nbsp;
and in creativity, fast decisions are really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:56.520 --> 01:02:02.600
stepping back as opposed to getting in the weeds.
Tim Ferriss: Can you say more about that? What I'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:02.600 --> 01:02:10.600
like to hear you riff on a little bit is how you&nbsp;
think about fast decisions. This is a source of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:10.600 --> 01:02:15.320
constant fascination for me. It's something I&nbsp;
revisit a lot. To what extent do I prioritize&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:15.320 --> 01:02:25.560
speed and just catalyzing things happening versus&nbsp;
honing, minimizing mistakes? I think that's what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:25.560 --> 01:02:32.080
it comes down to for a lot of people. It's what&nbsp;
error rate are you willing to accept? Is it 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:32.080 --> 01:02:35.920
percent? I know it's hard to track these things,&nbsp;
but is it, you're willing to accept a breakage&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:35.920 --> 01:02:42.520
of making the wrong call 10 percent of the time&nbsp;
because the speed net over time is just a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:42.520 --> 01:02:48.200
competitive advantage and good for what you're&nbsp;
doing? That's a long question, but with a fair&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:48.200 --> 01:02:51.840
amount of lead up. How do you think about that?
Chris Beresford-Hill: I remember that at the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:51.840 --> 01:02:54.858
of the day, it's subjective, that there is not —
Tim Ferriss: It's not mathematics.&nbsp;

01:02:54.858 --> 01:03:04.080
Chris Beresford-Hill: It's not mathematical. So&nbsp;
I've learned to trust myself, and I've learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:04.080 --> 01:03:13.000
that if you A/B something too long, you're lost.&nbsp;
So I think the more I get stuck in something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:13.000 --> 01:03:18.440
the more I realize I need to make a fast decision&nbsp;
because I don't think obsession when you're really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:18.440 --> 01:03:23.880
deciding go, no go, good idea, bad idea,&nbsp;
excited about this, not excited about this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:23.880 --> 01:03:27.720
I don't think time is your friend. I think time&nbsp;
pulls everything. It slows everything down. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:27.720 --> 01:03:32.480
takes the energy out of it. So I think&nbsp;
sometimes you've just got to let it fly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:32.480 --> 01:03:37.320
and then by the way, you can correct, and you&nbsp;
can change as you go. I mean, I've approved&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:37.320 --> 01:03:41.640
ads that wound up being completely different&nbsp;
ads that we're really proud of. So that's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:41.640 --> 01:03:49.440
other thing, is that nothing is totally fixed.
Tim Ferriss: It also strikes me that there are so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:49.440 --> 01:03:55.520
many things, just pulling from my own experience,&nbsp;
that get worse with time and more deliberation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:55.520 --> 01:04:03.600
like deals and just deal structures, negotiating.&nbsp;
Often in my experience, it's the longer it takes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:03.600 --> 01:04:10.880
the more that is a harbinger of pain to come or&nbsp;
just a bad wasteful outcome that ends up stalling&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:10.880 --> 01:04:18.280
out. There's so many things like that where it's&nbsp;
like, okay, if there's isn't some speed to this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:18.280 --> 01:04:21.000
likely the outcome is going to be worse.
Chris Beresford-Hill: For sure. Time kills&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:21.000 --> 01:04:27.000
all deals for sure, and it kills momentum, and it&nbsp;
kills energy, and it takes people. So if you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:27.000 --> 01:04:31.880
got people going, if you need to take something&nbsp;
offline for yourself, just know that their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:31.880 --> 01:04:37.160
muscles are going to cool. They're going to take&nbsp;
a break, and they're going to restart. Many times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:37.160 --> 01:04:43.160
the wrong decision can become the right decision.
If it's actually an okay time, I'd love to tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:43.160 --> 01:04:46.094
you a little bit about a Mountain Dew Super Bowl —
Tim Ferriss: Let's do it.&nbsp;

01:04:46.094 --> 01:04:49.160
Chris Beresford-Hill: — ad we did because&nbsp;
it started out as one thing and it became&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:49.160 --> 01:04:54.520
something totally different, and there's a&nbsp;
beauty in that sometimes the non-linear way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:54.520 --> 01:05:04.040
it really works. So this would be, I want to&nbsp;
say it's 2018. We are Mountain Dew's agency&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:04.040 --> 01:05:08.000
of record. I'm working at TBWA\Chiat\Day at the&nbsp;
time. I'm the chief creative officer, and I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:08.000 --> 01:05:13.720
these incredible executive creative directors&nbsp;
named Amy and Julia that run the Mountain Dew&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:13.720 --> 01:05:19.440
business. We got the Super Bowl brief, and the&nbsp;
idea we landed was something about how it was for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:19.440 --> 01:05:26.680
Mountain Dew Zero Sugar, and this is the zero that&nbsp;
they really got right. This one tastes so close to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:26.680 --> 01:05:32.320
the original that we needed to do something&nbsp;
that was going to cause a real reappraisal.&nbsp;

01:05:33.440 --> 01:05:36.520
Before we started talking, I was like, "That's&nbsp;
right" because I couldn't remember what it was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:36.520 --> 01:05:42.080
So our original idea was, it's impossible taste.&nbsp;
So that was the core thought we were executing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:42.080 --> 01:05:48.600
against. Amy and Julia had this really gutsy&nbsp;
idea, and the idea, and I'm caveating here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:48.600 --> 01:05:55.520
this idea never happened, was never purchased&nbsp;
by PepsiCo. So this is behind the scenes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:55.520 --> 01:06:00.680
our house at the agency. We had the&nbsp;
idea that for this impossible drink,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:00.680 --> 01:06:05.400
we were going to do the impossible Super Bowl ad.&nbsp;
So what we were going to do is we were going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:05.400 --> 01:06:12.440
announce mega press release billboards, front page&nbsp;
of Variety that Daniel Day-Lewis had agreed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:12.440 --> 01:06:19.720
star in Mountain Dew Zero Sugar's Super Bowl ad,&nbsp;
and we were going to do it without telling him.&nbsp;

01:06:19.720 --> 01:06:23.080
Tim Ferriss: Okay, continue. Yeah.
Chris Beresford-Hill: For sure, he would say&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:23.080 --> 01:06:28.600
"Go away" if he acknowledged it, and for sure he&nbsp;
would probably cease and desist, if not sue. Our&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:28.600 --> 01:06:32.800
whole idea was going to be we were going to let&nbsp;
this play, but we were going to say because this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:32.800 --> 01:06:37.680
Mountain Dew Zero Sugar was so impossibly good,&nbsp;
you deserve an impossibly hard-to-make Super Bowl&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:37.680 --> 01:06:43.800
ad, so we're going to do it. We were going to even&nbsp;
have a live cam of a limousine parked in front of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:43.800 --> 01:06:48.080
his last known address on the day of the Super&nbsp;
Bowl, and we were going to have the set ready,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:48.080 --> 01:06:51.760
and we were going to basically film and&nbsp;
live stream, is he going to come do it?&nbsp;

01:06:51.760 --> 01:06:55.800
So as I'm working with Amy and Julia on&nbsp;
this, we're loving it. We're having fun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:55.800 --> 01:06:59.560
and this is when — the best ideas are when you're&nbsp;
like, "We can never do this. We're going to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:59.560 --> 01:07:03.880
in big trouble. This is so wrong." When you feel&nbsp;
that, you've got to stay there. You absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:03.880 --> 01:07:07.240
have to. That's where all the interesting&nbsp;
stuff happens. So then we started saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:07.240 --> 01:07:12.440
"Okay, so what happens when he doesn't&nbsp;
show?" So we're talking. We're like, "Okay."&nbsp;

01:07:12.440 --> 01:07:17.840
So we made this up. The whole pre-campaign, all&nbsp;
the buzz, Daniel Day-Lewis sues Mountain Dew&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:17.840 --> 01:07:22.040
because he doesn't want to be part of this. He's&nbsp;
not going to participate, but we still had the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:22.040 --> 01:07:32.480
TV ad. So a camera winds through a set from There&nbsp;
Will Be Blood, and finds its way into this chapel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:32.480 --> 01:07:40.520
and finds the back of this cloaked character, and&nbsp;
it's Daniel Day-Lewis' character. Turns around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:40.520 --> 01:07:46.640
and it's Will Ferrell in a mustache and a hat,&nbsp;
and he does the monologue, or the beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:46.640 --> 01:07:51.760
performance piece of, "I take my straw and I&nbsp;
drink your milkshake," and he does. He just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:51.760 --> 01:07:55.480
goes nuts yelling about how "I take my straw,&nbsp;
and I drink your Mountain Dew Zero Sugar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:55.480 --> 01:08:00.760
and I drink it up." So then the rug pull&nbsp;
becomes insane. So he's not going to do it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:00.760 --> 01:08:05.960
and then Will Ferrell does an insane Daniel&nbsp;
Day-Lewis screaming about Mountain Dew Zero Sugar.&nbsp;

01:08:05.960 --> 01:08:11.960
So we know it's probably a long shot, but&nbsp;
we go to Pepsi. We go with a bunch of ideas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:11.960 --> 01:08:16.680
and we go — the client was a woman named Nicole,&nbsp;
and there's the CMO Pepsi guy named Greg Lyons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:16.680 --> 01:08:21.160
who's been there a long time, and he's great. He&nbsp;
knows creative. Again, when you talk about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:21.160 --> 01:08:25.480
kind of clients you can work with that understand&nbsp;
creative people but also understand their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:25.480 --> 01:08:31.560
business and can bridge that, that's why Pepsi&nbsp;
marketing has consistently been so strong. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:31.560 --> 01:08:36.960
we show Greg and Greg's like, "I love it. I love&nbsp;
it. There's no way we can do this." But he said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:36.960 --> 01:08:40.480
"But you know what? I'll bring it to our&nbsp;
legal because look, I love it. I just know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:40.480 --> 01:08:44.800
we can't do it. I love it." Sure enough, and I&nbsp;
won't even get into the myriad of reasons, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:44.800 --> 01:08:50.760
basically there was a potential legal precedent&nbsp;
that would've altered Pepsi stock's price forever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:50.760 --> 01:08:57.040
if we pissed off Daniel Day-Lewis or whatever.
Tim Ferriss: Hold on. That's too tantalizing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:57.040 --> 01:09:00.240
if you can't get into it, but just a little bit.
Chris Beresford-Hill: I'll give it. Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:00.240 --> 01:09:05.760
because nothing happened, nothing happened.&nbsp;
So I think there was a case that perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:05.760 --> 01:09:10.747
if this went wrong, he may be entitled to a&nbsp;
royalty on every Pepsi product sold forever.&nbsp;

01:09:10.747 --> 01:09:14.120
Tim Ferriss: I can see why legal would push back.
Chris Beresford-Hill: I'm talking about a bag of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:14.120 --> 01:09:18.600
Frito Lays in Bangkok is still Daniel Day-Lewis&nbsp;
gets money. It would have been the best thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:18.600 --> 01:09:21.600
that ever happened to him, but anyway.
Tim Ferriss: It's like that guy who had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:21.600 --> 01:09:24.707
phone thrown at him by Russell Crowe.
Chris Beresford-Hill: That's right.&nbsp;

01:09:24.707 --> 01:09:27.720
Tim Ferriss: Man won the lottery, Powerball.
Chris Beresford-Hill: And if he put that money&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:27.720 --> 01:09:31.658
into Apple stock, now how good is he doing?
Tim Ferriss: Okay.&nbsp;

01:09:31.658 --> 01:09:33.440
Chris Beresford-Hill: But [inaudible 01:21:53].
Tim Ferriss: So legal's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:33.440 --> 01:09:36.280
"I love it, we think not."
Chris Beresford-Hill: So we think not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:36.280 --> 01:09:41.000
but this is how it really happens. So we go back&nbsp;
to the office and we're — there's new ideas and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:41.000 --> 01:09:44.360
stuff, and we're sitting, the three of — Amy and&nbsp;
Julia are great. They're working with their teams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:44.360 --> 01:09:47.400
and then we would get together for&nbsp;
an hour or two, and we're saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:47.400 --> 01:09:52.920
"You know what?" There's just something about&nbsp;
that weird ass scene with the wrong actor just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:52.920 --> 01:09:59.560
screaming about Mountain Dew that we just loved.&nbsp;
We just loved it. So then you just go, "Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:59.560 --> 01:10:03.480
I don't know how you'd ever get to that without&nbsp;
the Daniel Day-Lewis thing, but let's cut off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:03.480 --> 01:10:11.840
Daniel Day-Lewis thing and make sense of this."
So then we said, "Okay, well what if it's about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:11.840 --> 01:10:18.560
Mountain Dew Zero Sugar is almost as good as the&nbsp;
original, or what if it's as good as the original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:18.560 --> 01:10:23.360
if not better and then we — that's our idea, and&nbsp;
we put the wrong person in an iconic scene, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:23.360 --> 01:10:28.120
have them go nuts," and make it just — because we&nbsp;
knew that — when we saw the Will Ferrell idea, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:28.120 --> 01:10:31.760
were like, "I just want to see that." So then we&nbsp;
knew, okay, if you have something people want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:31.760 --> 01:10:37.560
see, don't leave it. So then we said, "Okay, maybe&nbsp;
it's Cast Away. Maybe it's There Will Be Blood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:37.560 --> 01:10:44.720
Maybe it's The Shining," and that's how we landed&nbsp;
The Shining where we recast with Bryan Cranston&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:44.720 --> 01:10:50.000
and Tracee Ellis Ross remaking The Shining. But&nbsp;
again, he's ranting and looning about Mountain Dew&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:50.000 --> 01:10:55.480
Zero Sugar. So that's how we got to that. But we&nbsp;
would've never got to that if we didn't just go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:55.480 --> 01:11:00.800
a weird journey and stay creative the whole way.
Tim Ferriss: I want to come back to something you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:00.800 --> 01:11:09.360
said in passing that I'd love to hear more&nbsp;
about, and maybe you have another example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:09.360 --> 01:11:14.920
and this ties into Jeff Goodby. Make sure&nbsp;
you always feel like you're doing something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:14.920 --> 01:11:22.000
vaguely naughty. So that feeling of like, oh,&nbsp;
we're never going to get away with this, or ah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:22.000 --> 01:11:26.360
use slightly different wording. But could&nbsp;
you tie these things together because that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:26.360 --> 01:11:33.880
type of indicator, I want to understand what that&nbsp;
feeling is, and how you actually use it. Because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:33.880 --> 01:11:39.280
for instance, this is not a hundred percent, but&nbsp;
for me there is a certain physiological quickening&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:39.280 --> 01:11:45.240
that I experience, have experienced before&nbsp;
almost all of my best investments. There's an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:45.240 --> 01:11:50.640
obvious signal that I've learned to tune into.&nbsp;
It's not right a hundred percent of the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:50.640 --> 01:11:59.360
but the hit rate is very high. So what is this? Or&nbsp;
how do you use it? Any way you want to tackle it.&nbsp;

01:11:59.360 --> 01:12:04.720
Chris Beresford-Hill: The thing I love by the&nbsp;
way about asking people advice is they give you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:04.720 --> 01:12:11.720
advice, but people tell you who they are when they&nbsp;
give you advice too, and that's Jeff. Jeff is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:11.720 --> 01:12:17.040
super smart guy who went to Harvard and wrote for&nbsp;
The Harvard Lampoon. He happened into advertising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:17.040 --> 01:12:27.160
but he's just a vibey, really smart guy, and I&nbsp;
think he behaves like he wants to fuck around, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:27.160 --> 01:12:31.760
he's a serious businessman, and he can talk to the&nbsp;
CMO and CEO of any brand he works with and totally&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:31.760 --> 01:12:36.200
explain why he's doing what he's doing. But he&nbsp;
has this way about him where it's going to be fun.&nbsp;

01:12:36.200 --> 01:12:41.000
Some days you go to work, and your deadlines&nbsp;
are tough, and the client pressure is tough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:41.000 --> 01:12:45.440
but he just always behaved like it was fun,&nbsp;
like he was having fun whether he was or wasn't,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:45.440 --> 01:12:49.760
and that kind of vibe is great. That does&nbsp;
embolden you to say, "Okay, we should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:49.760 --> 01:12:55.440
thinking about this as a form of play." In the&nbsp;
day-to-day, you don't. It's about engineering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:55.440 --> 01:12:58.760
and it's about we have to get this just&nbsp;
right, and we have to hit this timeline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:58.760 --> 01:13:06.000
and we have to make everything neat and tidy.&nbsp;
But it's really our responsibility to make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:06.000 --> 01:13:10.840
closer to art because that's what people respond&nbsp;
to. People don't respond to tons of rational&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:10.840 --> 01:13:16.640
drivers. People don't respond to things that have&nbsp;
been focused group to death. It's our job to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:16.640 --> 01:13:25.560
the line and find the edge, and stay there. That&nbsp;
kind of attitude gives you that permission that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:25.560 --> 01:13:30.120
if you're feeling like this could be wrong,&nbsp;
you're probably onto something and if it feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:30.120 --> 01:13:35.200
dead right, you're probably dead in the water.
Tim Ferriss: Are there indicators for that? So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:35.200 --> 01:13:39.200
for instance, I'm making this up. But&nbsp;
you're going blue sky, you're coming up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:39.200 --> 01:13:46.360
with a lot of bad ideas, getting things out&nbsp;
of your head, so you can hopefully sift the —&nbsp;

01:13:46.360 --> 01:13:49.600
Chris Beresford-Hill: Sift the vomit.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, sift the vomit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:49.600 --> 01:13:54.120
Exactly. It's a good commercial visual for&nbsp;
you, somebody vomiting into their own gold&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:54.120 --> 01:14:04.360
pan over a river. In any case, in terms of doing&nbsp;
something wrong or that type of characteristic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:05.120 --> 01:14:10.240
it's a scent trail for something promising, what&nbsp;
are some indicators? Is it that people around you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:10.240 --> 01:14:15.760
are like, "Oh, God," or they break out laughing,&nbsp;
or somebody above you is like, "Oh, I love it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:15.760 --> 01:14:22.120
but I don't really want to say I love it." I don't&nbsp;
know how to really express what I'm trying to ask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:22.120 --> 01:14:24.400
but I think you get what I'm grasping for.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah, and there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:24.400 --> 01:14:28.920
different feelings. I was a very good kid, but&nbsp;
sometimes when you're presenting an idea, and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:28.920 --> 01:14:33.040
think you might get it through, if you feel like&nbsp;
maybe what you think you'd feel like if you were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:33.040 --> 01:14:38.320
shoplifting a Snickers bar, that's one tell is&nbsp;
when you're like, "Oh, boy, if I get out of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:38.320 --> 01:14:42.040
meeting…" So if you know you're getting away with&nbsp;
something, and if you feel that in your bones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:42.040 --> 01:14:47.760
that's something. If people are nervous, and they&nbsp;
start to scenario — "We all love the idea, but…"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:47.760 --> 01:14:51.520
they scenario plan. The other is if you just can't&nbsp;
get it out of your head, if you become a dog with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:51.520 --> 01:14:57.480
a bone, and us creatives, we are dying to get our&nbsp;
best ideas into the world. We become insufferable&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:58.040 --> 01:15:02.160
when we attach to one, and that actually is a&nbsp;
pretty good segue to the Adidas story if you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:02.160 --> 01:15:06.520
because that's one where we got away with one.
Tim Ferriss: So we are going to get to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:06.520 --> 01:15:13.440
Adidas. I'm going to put one thing in between,&nbsp;
which is — and you may have to help me with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:13.440 --> 01:15:17.867
pronunciation here. Tor Myhren.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yes.&nbsp;

01:15:17.867 --> 01:15:19.840
Tim Ferriss: Getting it?
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yep. All right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:19.840 --> 01:15:27.160
Executive at Apple, Apple CMO specifically, has&nbsp;
said what a world-class agency partner does is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:27.880 --> 01:15:30.187
"reduce things."
Yes.&nbsp;

01:15:30.187 --> 01:15:33.720
Tim Ferriss: What does this mean to you?
Chris Beresford-Hill: Well, it's the case&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:33.720 --> 01:15:44.560
for creative agencies because if I'm a soap&nbsp;
company, I could hire us, or I could just make&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:44.560 --> 01:15:50.160
our own ads. Well, I'd say, "Well, our soap's the&nbsp;
best soap, obviously, and we put love into it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:50.160 --> 01:15:55.720
our people…" you start to want to say everything,&nbsp;
and you want to say your Pollyanna version of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:55.720 --> 01:15:57.760
and I think an agency partner —
Tim Ferriss: I thought you said&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:57.760 --> 01:16:01.320
polyamorous version of it for a second.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah, Pollyanna&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:01.320 --> 01:16:05.747
version of it. No comment on&nbsp;
polyamour. That's a whole other topic.&nbsp;

01:16:05.747 --> 01:16:09.246
Tim Ferriss: We are in Austin, the&nbsp;
land of plenty. Anyway, continue.&nbsp;

01:16:09.246 --> 01:16:12.200
Chris Beresford-Hill: But I think what an agency&nbsp;
does is the clients come and say, "We want to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:12.200 --> 01:16:15.920
this, and this, and this, and we say, 'No, no&nbsp;
one's going to care.'" No one's going to care&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:15.920 --> 01:16:19.320
that you think your soap makers are the best&nbsp;
soap makers. No one's going to care that you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:19.320 --> 01:16:22.400
saying — everyone says the ingredient. Look,&nbsp;
we show you. Everyone says ingredients. So what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:22.400 --> 01:16:28.920
you get from us is your jumble of all the things&nbsp;
you want to do, and we strip it down to the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:28.920 --> 01:16:36.200
compelling core truth about why your product, or&nbsp;
service, or offering is compelling. Everyone adds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:36.200 --> 01:16:40.640
and really what you want from us is to&nbsp;
subtract, and come to you with the one thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:40.640 --> 01:16:44.640
and passionately and say, "We know, and we know&nbsp;
communication, and this is what you have to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:44.640 --> 01:16:50.200
to make your point." So it really is the art&nbsp;
of editing, and just down, down, down, down.&nbsp;

01:16:50.200 --> 01:16:55.120
Tim Ferriss: Any examples that come to mind?&nbsp;
I mean, this is not exactly a statement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:55.120 --> 01:16:59.520
but "Got Milk?" is pretty good. I&nbsp;
mean, it's an attention-grabbing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:59.520 --> 01:17:05.178
distillation for messaging purposes, but do&nbsp;
any other examples come to mind of reducing?&nbsp;

01:17:05.178 --> 01:17:07.200
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah, anything&nbsp;
good. I mean, anything good?&nbsp;

01:17:07.200 --> 01:17:09.960
Tim Ferriss: "A thousand songs in your pocket."
Chris Beresford-Hill: A thousand songs. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:09.960 --> 01:17:13.720
talk about a brilliant way to get at the benefit.
Tim Ferriss: Nobody gives a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:13.720 --> 01:17:16.400
shit how many megabytes.
Chris Beresford-Hill: It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:16.400 --> 01:17:18.280
not about speeds and feeds.
Tim Ferriss: Speeds and feeds.&nbsp;

01:17:18.280 --> 01:17:22.640
Chris Beresford-Hill: It's about, show me what&nbsp;
about this is magical. It's about finding the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:22.640 --> 01:17:28.120
one magical bit. I think you can just look at&nbsp;
any great execution or any great ad and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:28.120 --> 01:17:32.840
"Okay, that was focused, that was&nbsp;
reductive." That's the difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:32.840 --> 01:17:35.320
between the good ones and the bad ones.
Tim Ferriss: Inherently present.&nbsp;

01:17:35.320 --> 01:17:36.240
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: "Where's the beef?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:36.240 --> 01:17:37.787
We could go on and on.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah, exactly.&nbsp;

01:17:37.787 --> 01:17:40.347
Tim Ferriss: "Drivers wanted," on and on.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:17:40.347 --> 01:17:41.840
Tim Ferriss: All right, Adidas.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Okay. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:41.840 --> 01:17:47.067
is a good landing spot because it's like&nbsp;
the Mountain Dew one. It's a bit of a tale.&nbsp;

01:17:47.067 --> 01:17:49.440
Tim Ferriss: Love tales here.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Okay, good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:49.440 --> 01:17:55.920
I'll say every bit of it, as much to not get in&nbsp;
trouble, everything ended well for everybody. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:55.920 --> 01:18:06.040
start there. So we are given an Adidas assignment,&nbsp;
not the big brand Adidas assignment. Adidas works&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:06.040 --> 01:18:10.560
with a number of agencies on a number of projects,&nbsp;
but we were granted an awesome opportunity to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:10.560 --> 01:18:16.160
something for tennis for the US Open, what Adidas&nbsp;
calls Heartbeat Sports, which is tennis, fencing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:16.160 --> 01:18:22.000
swimming. So we had this opportunity and we&nbsp;
thought, "Okay, for our motley crew, for our squad&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:22.000 --> 01:18:26.560
to get to do something for a brand like Adidas,&nbsp;
we're going to show up at our very best." So we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:26.560 --> 01:18:30.827
invited the client, and I'll refer to the client&nbsp;
as the client. We invited the client to visit us.&nbsp;

01:18:30.827 --> 01:18:33.867
Tim Ferriss: Capital T, capital C.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yes.&nbsp;

01:18:33.867 --> 01:18:35.640
Tim Ferriss: The Client.
Chris Beresford-Hill: 18 point&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:35.640 --> 01:18:41.240
and a good client. We had the client in, and we&nbsp;
brought our strategist. We brought a strategist&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:41.240 --> 01:18:45.840
from another region that had worked on Adidas. We&nbsp;
brought a creative team. When a client is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:45.840 --> 01:18:49.880
tell you about a project, you don't normally bring&nbsp;
the creative team. Normally, you understand the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:49.880 --> 01:18:53.560
project, and decide what you want to do with it,&nbsp;
and then bring in the creative team. But we did.&nbsp;

01:18:53.560 --> 01:18:54.960
Tim Ferriss: Full-court press.
Chris Beresford-Hill: We went&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:54.960 --> 01:18:59.440
for the full-court press. By the way,&nbsp;
another lesson — certainly in advertising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:59.440 --> 01:19:05.280
but anywhere — is the more ground you can cover&nbsp;
in a room, the better because you start further&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:05.280 --> 01:19:09.200
ahead than if you constantly come together, and&nbsp;
split off, and come together, and split off. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:09.200 --> 01:19:12.840
our intention was, let's land a brief together.
Tim Ferriss: When you say land a brief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:12.840 --> 01:19:15.120
you're basically getting the gig, right?
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah, getting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:15.120 --> 01:19:18.840
gig and also — and the strategy, and what we&nbsp;
want to do, and what our objective is, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:18.840 --> 01:19:23.000
challenge that we're going to meet with&nbsp;
creative ideas. So we're having this day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:23.000 --> 01:19:30.480
and we're talking a lot about how — I think the&nbsp;
statistic is still valid, but something like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:30.480 --> 01:19:36.040
80 percent at the time of young girls would drop&nbsp;
out of sports due to body confidence issues or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:36.040 --> 01:19:40.960
not feeling like athletics were for them because&nbsp;
obviously the kind of media biases towards male&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:40.960 --> 01:19:45.720
athletes. So an initiative not only with Adidas,&nbsp;
but also with Nike, and all these companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:45.720 --> 01:19:51.240
is about keeping young girls in sports because&nbsp;
it obviously has great benefits, not only that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:51.240 --> 01:19:55.080
they could go on to be professional athletes,&nbsp;
but for confidence, and all kinds of teamwork,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:55.080 --> 01:19:58.800
and all the great things that come with it.
So our client was excited about the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:58.800 --> 01:20:03.120
of let's do something about girls in sport.&nbsp;
Okay, we're talking and then our client says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:03.120 --> 01:20:06.800
"We have Billie Jean King, and she's going to&nbsp;
work with us." We're thinking, "Billie Jean King&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:06.800 --> 01:20:13.760
is awesome." I mean, she's a total rule-breaker,&nbsp;
trailblazer. She played the battle of the sexes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:13.760 --> 01:20:18.040
so how exciting. So we had the very obvious&nbsp;
idea, we're like, okay, for the US Open,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:18.040 --> 01:20:21.360
we've got to take her blue suede shoes, and&nbsp;
we've got to bring them back. We've got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:21.360 --> 01:20:26.520
bring them back and sell them. So obvious, dumb,&nbsp;
done. And then as it turns out, it's a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:26.520 --> 01:20:30.120
more complicated than that. They've got to make&nbsp;
that in a factory somewhere else in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:30.120 --> 01:20:35.760
and then ship that volume of shoes on a big&nbsp;
slow ship, and so that wasn't going to happen.&nbsp;

01:20:35.760 --> 01:20:41.560
So one of the creators we brought into the room,&nbsp;
his name is Ricardo Franco, he says, "Why don't we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:41.560 --> 01:20:48.600
just change people's shoes into Billie Jean King's&nbsp;
shoes?" It was just that, and we went, "Okay, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:48.600 --> 01:20:53.000
sounds interesting. That sounds like something&nbsp;
I haven't heard before," and we didn't know what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:53.000 --> 01:21:00.080
it meant. We said, "Okay, so would we have felt&nbsp;
applications that we could stick on?" So if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:00.080 --> 01:21:05.280
came in wearing a pair of New Balance, you could&nbsp;
fix them. We kept talking and said, "What about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:05.280 --> 01:21:08.760
spray paint? What if we get shoe artists? There's&nbsp;
these people like the Shoe Surgeon and all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:08.760 --> 01:21:11.880
people. What if we got some of those people?"
And so we started saying, "Okay, maybe this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:11.880 --> 01:21:15.000
possible. We should figure this out." And&nbsp;
even someone in the room said, "Oh, God,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:15.000 --> 01:21:19.440
imagine if someone comes in a pair of Nikes,"&nbsp;
and everyone was rubbing their hands together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:19.440 --> 01:21:24.320
This was great. So a great lesson is when&nbsp;
you get what you want, get out of the room.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:24.320 --> 01:21:27.827
Don't let the vibe dip, and you start thinking&nbsp;
about all the reasons why it might not work.&nbsp;

01:21:27.827 --> 01:21:30.280
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, don't oversell it.
Chris Beresford-Hill: So I've learned this lesson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:30.280 --> 01:21:35.120
as many of us have, and seen it in practice.
So leaving the room, normally we'd say, "Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:35.120 --> 01:21:38.720
we'll come back in two weeks, and let's put it in&nbsp;
a deck, and let's draw out the comp." But in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:38.720 --> 01:21:44.760
one, we just knew that the more we sat on it, time&nbsp;
would kill this deal. Time would give people time&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:44.760 --> 01:21:51.600
to think about the pros and cons. So our team was&nbsp;
just, "Go do it. Just go. We've got money." Nancy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:51.600 --> 01:21:56.280
our president in New York, loved the idea. So&nbsp;
we said, "Can we just get 10 grand to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:56.280 --> 01:21:59.600
paying people to test? Don't get client&nbsp;
money, let's just do it." So we started&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:59.600 --> 01:22:02.320
setting this whole thing up and we were —
Tim Ferriss: That's an important point.&nbsp;

01:22:02.320 --> 01:22:03.200
Chris Beresford-Hill: You've got to have —
Tim Ferriss: Let's get a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:03.200 --> 01:22:05.547
internal money just to —
Chris Beresford-Hill: Start it.&nbsp;

01:22:05.547 --> 01:22:07.000
Tim Ferriss: Sort of jump the gun.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah, let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:07.000 --> 01:22:11.520
make progress because we saw a green light. This&nbsp;
is not the kind of idea that gets green-lit. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:11.520 --> 01:22:16.600
had a stolen Snickers in my pocket, and I wanted&nbsp;
out of that store. So anyway, we start R&amp;Ding it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:16.600 --> 01:22:21.440
and we find someone that blends a cool pearl&nbsp;
blue paint, and we make these stencils and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:21.440 --> 01:22:26.200
start doing these tests, and we have — so we're&nbsp;
spray painting Converse and Air Jordans, and we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:26.200 --> 01:22:30.200
doing them around the agency. They look awesome.&nbsp;
I mean, anyone who sees them goes, "Holy shit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:30.200 --> 01:22:35.720
what is that? I want that. I'm so into that."
So we get maybe about a week and a half away&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:35.720 --> 01:22:41.560
from the US Open, and this client didn't live in&nbsp;
the States. It was a global client. The phone call&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:41.560 --> 01:22:49.520
comes, and I knew the phone call was coming. We&nbsp;
get on the phone and the client says, "I just want&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:49.520 --> 01:22:55.200
you to know though, that we can only have people&nbsp;
wearing Adidas shoes, that we can spray any Adidas&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:55.200 --> 01:22:59.680
shoe you want, but we can only have people&nbsp;
spray Adidas shoes." So at this moment, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:59.680 --> 01:23:06.720
is a moment where, no, because then it&nbsp;
takes all the coolness out of the idea.&nbsp;

01:23:06.720 --> 01:23:13.920
But if I say no now, I mean, she's got a&nbsp;
week and a half to lock us out of everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:13.920 --> 01:23:18.600
and this is the client. They hold the purse&nbsp;
strings. And they have the ultimate say. They're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:18.600 --> 01:23:26.920
the brand. So I can't argue it because if I lose&nbsp;
the argument, it's over. So I lied. And so I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:26.920 --> 01:23:35.680
"Absolutely," and I knew what I was doing. I knew&nbsp;
that I was straddling a third rail where if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:35.680 --> 01:23:40.680
do something like this and it doesn't work out&nbsp;
for you, it could be very bad for my network's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:40.680 --> 01:23:44.538
relationship with that giant company.
Tim Ferriss: To say the least.&nbsp;

01:23:44.538 --> 01:23:48.840
Chris Beresford-Hill: But there comes a moment&nbsp;
where it's not even, is it worth it? You say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:48.840 --> 01:23:57.640
"Okay, I understand lawyers are there to avert&nbsp;
risk." But in my gut, I know that when someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:57.640 --> 01:24:02.840
at Nike sees a shoe of theirs defaced&nbsp;
to look like a Billie Jean King shoe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:02.840 --> 01:24:09.400
I know as a human being that that brand will&nbsp;
not come after Billie Jean King's legacy or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:09.400 --> 01:24:16.080
empowering young girls in sport. I just knew it.&nbsp;
Now the lawyer would not sign it, but I knew it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:16.080 --> 01:24:22.800
So that's the moment where you say, "Fuck it."
So our team is still going, and our client is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:22.800 --> 01:24:29.360
at the US Open. Our client is still in Europe. So&nbsp;
I'm in Beverly Hills at the Hilton, at the Beverly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:29.360 --> 01:24:37.080
Hilton with my partner Nancy, my president, and we&nbsp;
are filming a big campaign for Hilton. The morning&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:37.080 --> 01:24:42.560
of the first day of the US Open, my phone buzzes&nbsp;
