01:26:09.320 --> 01:26:12.440
So I said, "I will have our PR reach out to&nbsp;
all the publications and ask them to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:12.440 --> 01:26:15.520
it down because I know a thing or two about&nbsp;
journalists, and journalists will say, 'No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:15.520 --> 01:26:18.360
thanks. It happened, so I'm going to cover what&nbsp;
I want.'" But I said, "Look, we're going to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:18.360 --> 01:26:25.000
that." I said, "I'm very sorry, and I understand&nbsp;
you're very angry with me." So it happens, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:25.000 --> 01:26:34.440
then the little Hypebeast community love it, and&nbsp;
then it all goes away, and there was no problem.&nbsp;

01:26:34.440 --> 01:26:38.760
People thought it was really cool, and they got&nbsp;
some tip-of-the-spear cred for doing something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:38.760 --> 01:26:42.800
really innovative. All the hardcore sneaker&nbsp;
nerds loved it, and they started going for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:42.800 --> 01:26:49.600
thousands of dollars on eBay, and we made a&nbsp;
great case study out of it. That client was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:49.600 --> 01:26:54.760
included in juries to evaluate the biggest brand&nbsp;
ideas of the year for notoriety for the highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:54.760 --> 01:27:00.360
disruptive Billie Jean King, your shoes idea.
So the lesson there, or not even the lesson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

