McDonald's because it's just so hard after all&nbsp;
that to sit down and then hammer it out. And you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:03.560 --> 01:39:06.920
know as a writer, and I know as a writer sometimes&nbsp;
you're like, "I know I have to do it, but I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:06.920 --> 01:39:11.840
want to." So even watching him bribe himself,&nbsp;
watching one of the most dynamic, prolific,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:11.840 --> 01:39:18.080
edgy writers, he has to fool himself into it. And&nbsp;
by the way, sometimes when we make commercials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:18.080 --> 01:39:22.040
we have to make little animatics, like little&nbsp;
boardomatics, like rips. That's considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:22.040 --> 01:39:27.680
a chore. That whole show is a boardomatics when&nbsp;
you watch how they make it. So it's so inspiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:27.680 --> 01:39:33.320
to see those two power through. And there's&nbsp;
just a ton of stuff that you can relate to.&nbsp;

01:39:33.320 --> 01:39:39.080
The two other ones I love are, I love the&nbsp;
Metallica doc Some Kind of Monster because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:39.080 --> 01:39:45.240
it gets into doing creative within a team and&nbsp;
chemistry and how challenging that can be. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:45.240 --> 01:39:49.920
that documents Metallica almost breaking up&nbsp;
and working out their shit as they come up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:49.920 --> 01:39:54.120
with The Black Album. And it's amazing. And&nbsp;
you don't have to like Metallica to love it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:54.120 --> 01:40:00.680
to see are you more of a Lars? Are you more of&nbsp;
a James? Who do you side with here? So that's an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:00.680 --> 01:40:06.600
amazing study on high-performing creative team&nbsp;
dynamics. And then the last one, and the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:06.600 --> 01:40:11.098
underrated is Conan O'Brien Can't Stop.
Tim Ferriss: Okay.&nbsp;

01:40:11.098 --> 01:40:16.120
Chris Beresford-Hill: So he made — after that&nbsp;
really painful departure from The Tonight Show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:16.120 --> 01:40:22.120
he had a non-compete. So he threw a comedy&nbsp;
tour, and so he allowed it to be documented.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:22.120 --> 01:40:27.160
And you see that this guy is a creative animal&nbsp;
that coming out of what he just came out of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:27.160 --> 01:40:32.640
where you must be so damaged and wounded, but&nbsp;
he throws himself right into another enterprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:32.640 --> 01:40:37.160
and they're writing bits and skits and they're&nbsp;
staying up late at night the night before a show&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:37.160 --> 01:40:42.160
to rewrite it. And just seeing the obsession&nbsp;
in the craft of it, you're like, I watch the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:42.160 --> 01:40:46.280
thing and I'm like, "I do not work hard enough."
And you see the euphoria, the euphoria of, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:46.280 --> 01:40:50.000
slap happiness around all of it when you really&nbsp;
push yourself to — that man really pushes himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:50.000 --> 01:40:53.680
to his limits. And you watch that and you're like,&nbsp;
I just want to go and write something after I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:53.680 --> 01:40:59.480
see that. Those things, any one of those things,&nbsp;
they inspire me so much that I've got a bank that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:59.480 --> 01:41:02.440
someone else will get something out of them.
Tim Ferriss: I can't wait to watch Some Kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:02.440 --> 01:41:09.120
of Monster and Can't Stop. I've seen the&nbsp;
