01:41:09.120 --> 01:41:11.960
part? Where did I laugh hardest?
Chris Beresford-Hill: Well, Tim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:11.960 --> 01:41:18.067
I don't want to be bawdy, but would it be&nbsp;
the Apple user agreement? Human Centipede?&nbsp;

01:41:18.067 --> 01:41:23.258
Tim Ferriss: It was the voiceover&nbsp;
session that was related to that.&nbsp;

01:41:23.258 --> 01:41:23.874
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yes.
Tim Ferriss: There's a lot of good stuff.&nbsp;

01:41:23.874 --> 01:41:25.340
Chris Beresford-Hill: They were in a booth.
Tim Ferriss: There's a lot of good stuff.&nbsp;

01:41:25.340 --> 01:41:28.600
Chris Beresford-Hill: It's crazy stuff.
Tim Ferriss: Wow. Hey, Whipple, Squeeze This.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:28.600 --> 01:41:33.840
I haven't thought about that in ages. I used to&nbsp;
have that book when I was really cutting my teeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:33.840 --> 01:41:41.640
on copy and spending a lot of my own money doing&nbsp;
very expensive testing because the lead time and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:41.640 --> 01:41:48.800
the turnaround time for any type of quantitative&nbsp;
feedback on magazine ads, I mean, oh, my God, how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:48.800 --> 01:41:56.880
painful. I had that book deliberately cover-out&nbsp;
on my shelf as a reminder of some of the contents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:56.880 --> 01:42:03.000
It's been a very, very long time, but it's wild&nbsp;
that that has now resurfaced in my life. What fun.&nbsp;

01:42:03.000 --> 01:42:08.240
When it comes to the prompts and the questions,&nbsp;
are there any other questions that come to mind?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:08.240 --> 01:42:14.400
If you're stuck and you're just like, man, we've&nbsp;
been banging our head against the wall, we're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:14.400 --> 01:42:20.560
there. And this is going to lead into some of the&nbsp;
less glamorous maybe aspects of the job where it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:20.560 --> 01:42:26.600
like, okay, you've got to come up with X number of&nbsp;
ideas, this is important and you're getting close&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:26.600 --> 01:42:35.320
to deadline and you don't have it. What do you&nbsp;
do to mix things up? And I suppose just broadly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:35.320 --> 01:42:38.880
speaking, what do you do in a situation like that?
Chris Beresford-Hill: Well, writer's block doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:38.880 --> 01:42:46.280
really exist. You think it's blocked, but clearly&nbsp;
you can. It really is like a game of tricking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:46.280 --> 01:42:53.000
yourself. So, say, Sally is trying to come up with&nbsp;
ideas. "Sally, what are your five favorite movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:53.000 --> 01:42:59.080
or directors or writers? Okay, what would each&nbsp;
one of them do?" It's just coming up with a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:59.080 --> 01:43:04.720
challenge or a new way to approach it that fools&nbsp;
you into coughing something up. And then again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:04.720 --> 01:43:08.800
I'll go as far as then what's the absolute right&nbsp;
thing to do? What's the absolute wrong thing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:08.800 --> 01:43:14.400
do? What would I do if I thought this whole thing&nbsp;
was a big joke, if I wanted to make fun of it?&nbsp;

01:43:14.400 --> 01:43:19.160
And again, and then even the cause technique of&nbsp;
just start writing sentences and finish them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:19.160 --> 01:43:24.400
But it's not even — I think it's knowing yourself&nbsp;
and knowing your interests and then using avenues.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:24.400 --> 01:43:28.720
So what are the things you're into? Put it through&nbsp;
that lens. But it's just, it's a forcing exercise&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:28.720 --> 01:43:33.120
and it's really uncomfortable. It's really,&nbsp;
really uncomfortable because the more you get out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:33.120 --> 01:43:36.520
you will find — one thing I have found, and&nbsp;
I don't think it's just me, I've talked to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:36.520 --> 01:43:44.560
friends about it, is that four-hour, five-hour,&nbsp;
whatever your big dump is, later you will have new&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:44.560 --> 01:43:49.160
ideas and things. But the biggest treasure trove&nbsp;
you'll ever have is when the challenge is fresh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:49.160 --> 01:43:53.280
when your brain is first trying to put it&nbsp;
together. And the more you get out of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:53.280 --> 01:43:57.480
moment, the more you're paid back later.
Tim Ferriss: What do you do when you're,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:57.480 --> 01:44:03.320
say, overwhelmed or unfocused? Because this&nbsp;
is a game of creativity and we're all playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:03.320 --> 01:44:07.480
different games. So all of our careers on&nbsp;
some level are games of different types of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:07.480 --> 01:44:15.120
rules. Some you can bend, some you can break,&nbsp;
but endurance is important. The ability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:15.120 --> 01:44:21.560
not just flame out burning the candle at both&nbsp;
ends. Some people are superhuman and can bank&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:21.560 --> 01:44:26.360
all sorts of crazy work weeks and sleep four&nbsp;
hours a night because they happen to have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:26.360 --> 01:44:32.218
right genetic profile for that. But what do&nbsp;
you do when you're overwhelmed or unfocused?&nbsp;

01:44:32.218 --> 01:44:34.480
Chris Beresford-Hill: Well, interesting that&nbsp;
you bring that up because I work with some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:34.480 --> 01:44:40.200
those people that are, I don't know if they&nbsp;
sleep and I am sensitive and I need sleep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:40.200 --> 01:44:45.040
And so I'm very aware that I'm not like a&nbsp;
full beast. So there's two actually, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:45.040 --> 01:44:49.080
question as you phrase it, there's kind of two&nbsp;
answers that are different, but I read a book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:49.080 --> 01:44:54.080
I know you know this book called Bird by Bird.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, so good. An amazing book.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:54.080 --> 01:45:01.080
And some people will ignore it because it's&nbsp;
intended at face value for fiction writers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:01.080 --> 01:45:04.480
It does not matter. It does not matter.
Chris Beresford-Hill: So I had to read it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:04.480 --> 01:45:07.867
at school, so talking about… I must've had a&nbsp;
good teacher or whatever, so I had to read it.&nbsp;

01:45:07.867 --> 01:45:09.760
Tim Ferriss: What a gift.
Chris Beresford-Hill: I don't remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:09.760 --> 01:45:16.600
a single thing in the book except for the titular&nbsp;
story. And that story was, and I think about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:16.600 --> 01:45:22.520
all the time. And so the author's little brother&nbsp;
had to write a report on the birds and he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:22.520 --> 01:45:27.360
books open and printed out essays everywhere.&nbsp;
And he was in a full meltdown and panic.&nbsp;

01:45:27.360 --> 01:45:29.238
Tim Ferriss: He pushed it&nbsp;
off until the last minute.&nbsp;

01:45:29.238 --> 01:45:29.880
Chris Beresford-Hill: Pushed&nbsp;
it off until the last minute.&nbsp;

01:45:29.880 --> 01:45:31.967
Tim Ferriss: He had all semester&nbsp;
to do it, something like that.&nbsp;

01:45:31.967 --> 01:45:34.680
Chris Beresford-Hill: Of course, that whole&nbsp;
thing, everyone has a version of that and he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:34.680 --> 01:45:39.120
there. And I think in my version of the story,&nbsp;
he's crying at the table with all that stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:39.120 --> 01:45:44.840
and then good old Dad comes in and goes, "Just&nbsp;
take it bird by bird, bud, take it bird by bird."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:44.840 --> 01:45:50.640
And you're like, "Wow." So I have an imaginary&nbsp;
father that comes in and says that to me every&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:50.640 --> 01:45:57.200
time because it's permission, permission to stop&nbsp;
thinking about the totality of it and permission&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:57.200 --> 01:46:02.520
to do the littlest bit. The other quotation&nbsp;
that I love, I don't have a ton of quotations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:02.520 --> 01:46:10.480
but the great Arthur Ashe said, "Start where&nbsp;
you are. Use what you have. Do what you can."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:10.480 --> 01:46:15.880
And that's so fantastically empowering because&nbsp;
it's saying that you're in a good enough spot and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:15.880 --> 01:46:21.280
you're off the hook for anything more than that.
But then the other part of it about being a beast&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:21.280 --> 01:46:25.960
and the constant pressure is I've&nbsp;
figured out that I can't meditate.&nbsp;

01:46:25.960 --> 01:46:30.840
I think I'm too, it's something I'll get to&nbsp;
later in life, but I think I'm too — I can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:30.840 --> 01:46:35.880
sit still. I can't be alone in my thoughts. And&nbsp;
so I discovered that instead of turning the dial&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:35.880 --> 01:46:41.320
down to zero, I found also that hard fitness is&nbsp;
another meditative instead of going down to zero,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:41.320 --> 01:46:47.360
it just puts me at a 10. And so every morning&nbsp;
I go do a similar workout that gives me almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:47.360 --> 01:46:53.040
like an existential crisis so that by the&nbsp;
time I show up at work, whatever this kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:53.040 --> 01:46:58.160
of fog of anxiety that I used to live in,&nbsp;
it just burns it off and I just am so calm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:58.160 --> 01:47:01.387
for the rest of the day because I've already&nbsp;
been through something tough in the morning.&nbsp;

01:47:01.387 --> 01:47:02.880
Tim Ferriss: What is the workout?
Chris Beresford-Hill: And that's helping. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:02.880 --> 01:47:06.080
a gym in New York City called Tone House&nbsp;
and there's just one of them. And I used&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:06.080 --> 01:47:10.040
to exercise in college and I was in pretty&nbsp;
good shape. And then I got to doing a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:10.040 --> 01:47:18.400
writing and drinking beer and doing writing, and&nbsp;
I kind of let it slip. And I think around 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:18.400 --> 01:47:23.160
back when people would upload Facebook albums&nbsp;
the night after you did anything, I went to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:23.160 --> 01:47:27.640
New Year's party and there was a picture of me&nbsp;
on New Year's Day and I was leaned over a table&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:27.640 --> 01:47:32.320
of food and I swear in my head I looked like&nbsp;
Alfred Hitchcock or something. It was like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:32.320 --> 01:47:38.200
way my jacket lapel was open came out on this&nbsp;
huge thing and I got so upset that I looked up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:38.200 --> 01:47:44.360
"Hardest workout in New York" and then, bing,&nbsp;
there was this one-off gym called Tone House.&nbsp;

01:47:44.360 --> 01:47:50.080
And I went that day, on New Year's Day they were&nbsp;
open and the class is like — the founder is a guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:50.080 --> 01:47:55.240
named Alonzo Wilson, and he played D1 football and&nbsp;
played a little pro football. And when he stopped&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:55.240 --> 01:47:58.320
playing, he missed the practices. So it's a lot of&nbsp;
— the way I describe it to friends is it's like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:58.320 --> 01:48:02.320
one-hour Under Armour commercial that you're in&nbsp;
where you're just dropping the deck and jumping&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:02.320 --> 01:48:07.760
over stuff and banging ropes and the heart rate&nbsp;
stays up the whole time. And so the first time I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:07.760 --> 01:48:12.960
did it, I stepped out of class after 10 minutes,&nbsp;
because the warm warm-up is really intense cardio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:12.960 --> 01:48:17.080
and I vomited in the locker room and I stayed up&nbsp;
at night staring at the ceiling and being like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:17.080 --> 01:48:19.680
"I can't let this thing get me." And&nbsp;
so I came back the week later and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:19.680 --> 01:48:24.760
week later and it kind of pulled me in.
And now me and a fairly regular group are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:24.760 --> 01:48:30.960
there five days a week, six days a week, and then&nbsp;
we do other stuff. But that has been such a gift&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:30.960 --> 01:48:37.160
for me because I'm just — I used to walk around in&nbsp;
between meetings and I'd be like, "Should I run?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:37.160 --> 01:48:42.760
Am I using my time well?" I would just ruminate&nbsp;
on all the things I could be doing, wasn't doing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:42.760 --> 01:48:47.440
And something about really beating myself up in&nbsp;
the morning, I don't expend the energy worrying&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:47.440 --> 01:48:52.467
about all those things. I just focus on what&nbsp;
I'm focusing on. So it's made me normal.&nbsp;

01:48:52.467 --> 01:48:53.787
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Do you relate?&nbsp;

01:48:53.787 --> 01:49:00.920
Tim Ferriss: You're inspiring me to double down.&nbsp;
I've recently, knock on wood, largely addressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:00.920 --> 01:49:05.880
this kind of crippling lower back issue that has&nbsp;
sidelined me for the better part of the last year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:05.880 --> 01:49:12.880
And I'm really excited to get back into serious&nbsp;
exercise. And this might sound strange because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:12.880 --> 01:49:16.080
I'm not built like a border collie, but it's&nbsp;
kind of like if you can't have a working dog&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:16.080 --> 01:49:21.200
and put that dog in an apartment all day long,&nbsp;
the dog's going to go crazy. And I remember when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:21.200 --> 01:49:30.880
I was training my dog Molly and taking it super&nbsp;
seriously, going full deep dive on dog training.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:30.880 --> 01:49:36.280
And one of the lines that stuck with me is "A&nbsp;
tired dog is a happy dog." I think that is true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:36.280 --> 01:49:40.240
not for every person, but for people who have a&nbsp;
certain hard wiring and sounds like you're one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:40.240 --> 01:49:48.200
of them, I'm certainly one of them. If you're&nbsp;
not putting that creature to work physically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:48.200 --> 01:49:50.894
all sorts of strange things are going&nbsp;
to creep out of the cracks, mentally.&nbsp;

01:49:50.894 --> 01:49:55.080
Chris Beresford-Hill: And not the best things&nbsp;
because you want to get a lot done and you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:55.080 --> 01:49:58.640
to be tired out a little bit to be a little&nbsp;
bit normal about it because if you're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:58.640 --> 01:50:01.800
full energy, you're doing what's in front&nbsp;
of you and you're freaking out about all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:01.800 --> 01:50:05.800
other things you could, should, might. And when&nbsp;
you're just a little taken back a little bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:05.800 --> 01:50:09.800
you're, I'm more present. It's great.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I think a lot of kids end&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:09.800 --> 01:50:15.280
up getting into trouble in school because they&nbsp;
have idle cycles. They don't know what to, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:15.280 --> 01:50:19.720
they don't know how to apply those idle&nbsp;
cycles. I remember in school, for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:19.720 --> 01:50:24.120
it's like I needed to draw nonstop in&nbsp;
grade school. I got very good grades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:24.120 --> 01:50:30.560
I did really well, but I would get so bored in&nbsp;
class. I had to draw all the time, even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:30.560 --> 01:50:35.640
I was listening to exactly what the teacher was&nbsp;
saying because I had to occupy myself. And I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:35.640 --> 01:50:39.920
fortunate that I'd somehow chanced upon drawing,&nbsp;
but a lot of other kids who were very smart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:39.920 --> 01:50:45.160
got into all sorts of trouble, got kicked out, got&nbsp;
suspended, ended up getting involved drugs because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:45.160 --> 01:50:52.480
they didn't know how to occupy or consume those&nbsp;
cycles. And exercise is an amazing way to do it.&nbsp;

01:50:52.480 --> 01:50:57.440
Any other books, favorite books that you&nbsp;
have or put a different way, books that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:57.440 --> 01:51:02.009
you have gifted or recommended a lot to other&nbsp;
people outside of what we've discussed already?&nbsp;

01:51:02.009 --> 01:51:04.920
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yes, and I love&nbsp;
this question because I find those are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:04.920 --> 01:51:09.880
the things I always jot down when I hear&nbsp;
what people gift. Two of them. One of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:09.880 --> 01:51:14.960
is called The Easy Way to Quit Smoking&nbsp;
by Allen Carr, have you heard of this?&nbsp;

01:51:14.960 --> 01:51:19.960
Tim Ferriss: That's so weird. All of these things&nbsp;
are coming up in this conversation, I listened to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:22.840 --> 01:51:29.080
a derivative of that, but it's based on the same&nbsp;
method, The Easy Way to Quit Caffeine. I listened&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:29.080 --> 01:51:33.680
to the audiobook, somebody had recommended it to&nbsp;
me and I stopped. I went 30 or 40 days without&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:33.680 --> 01:51:37.560
any caffeine, which is the first time I've&nbsp;
done that since I was like, God only knows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:37.560 --> 01:51:41.480
16? So please keep going. But this is —
Chris Beresford-Hill: In my 20s, I smoked&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:41.480 --> 01:51:45.560
cigarettes and I was hopelessly addicted to&nbsp;
them. And I don't know how I found the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:45.560 --> 01:51:50.160
but I read it and it told me bit by bit all the&nbsp;
reasons why I thought I had to keep or why I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:50.160 --> 01:51:54.560
hang on to it. And it dispelled them one by one&nbsp;
and it was kind of romantic. And then there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:54.560 --> 01:51:58.280
chapter and it says, "Go outside and have your&nbsp;
last cigarette." And I went outside and I did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:58.280 --> 01:52:03.000
and then I finished the book and I never smoked&nbsp;
again. And I talk about it sparingly because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:03.000 --> 01:52:07.280
the easy way is kind of usually a red flag of&nbsp;
anything, but that's what I love about it. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:07.280 --> 01:52:12.200
you look it up on Amazon, the reviews are in,&nbsp;
it is like five-star for thousands, but I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:12.200 --> 01:52:16.864
given it to four or five friends and all four or&nbsp;
five of them just read it and never smoked again.&nbsp;

01:52:16.864 --> 01:52:17.440
Tim Ferriss: So wild.
Chris Beresford-Hill: So that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:17.440 --> 01:52:22.360
book is probably a life-saving book. And then&nbsp;
the other one that I love, and I know you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:22.360 --> 01:52:27.280
this book, but The Culture Code by Daniel Coyle.
Tim Ferriss: I know the book and I have not read&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:27.280 --> 01:52:30.760
it and it's been on my to-read list forever.
Chris Beresford-Hill: I think you would not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:30.760 --> 01:52:35.400
only love the book, but you would love talking&nbsp;
to Daniel about that book because I don't like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:35.400 --> 01:52:41.080
business books and especially when things become&nbsp;
dense and theoretical, I get lost. But he went&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:41.080 --> 01:52:47.280
and spent time in 10 incredible high-performing&nbsp;
cultures that are all different from IDEO to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:47.280 --> 01:52:52.720
the San Antonio Spurs to the Navy SEALs, and just&nbsp;
identified what makes people feel like they belong&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:52.720 --> 01:52:56.680
in those and what gives people the psychological&nbsp;
safety to do their best, do their best in those&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:56.680 --> 01:53:00.880
environments. And you read it and you'll get&nbsp;
confirmation bias. You'll be like, I do that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:00.880 --> 01:53:04.907
That's great. Or you're reminded I need to do&nbsp;
that. Or there's things you never thought of.&nbsp;

01:53:04.907 --> 01:53:09.040
Tim Ferriss: Now, do you recommend this book&nbsp;
to people who are individual contributors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:09.040 --> 01:53:12.680
kind of operators on the front lines,&nbsp;
or do you give that to people who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:12.680 --> 01:53:14.920
running companies? Who do you give that to?
Chris Beresford-Hill: Good question. I mean, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:14.920 --> 01:53:20.320
think for leadership for sure. So if anyone's&nbsp;
stepping into management, but I also find it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:20.320 --> 01:53:25.120
good — I would imagine I would've loved the book&nbsp;
if I wasn't a manager just to understand the kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:25.120 --> 01:53:31.280
of culture that I want to be part of. Obviously,&nbsp;
we're all students all the time, so I think there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:31.280 --> 01:53:35.640
wouldn't be any harm in imagining if you were&nbsp;
to lead something or if you were to have total&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:35.640 --> 01:53:38.960
control, what kind of culture you'd want. But&nbsp;
definitely if you're in leadership or you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:38.960 --> 01:53:43.520
stepping into leadership or if you're stepping&nbsp;
into a new role, there's just the simplest&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:43.520 --> 01:53:48.640
things you could do to show that everyone you&nbsp;
meet, that there's a long-term potential future&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:48.640 --> 01:53:52.960
