01:57:00.480 --> 01:57:05.480
it's always the best a hundred dollars I spend&nbsp;
every week. It's the best thing. And I'll never&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:05.480 --> 01:57:10.907
change it. And even if I'm down to my last hundred&nbsp;
dollars, I will go have breakfast with someone.&nbsp;

01:57:10.907 --> 01:57:13.538
Tim Ferriss: It's a great location. I haven't&nbsp;
been in a million years, but that is —&nbsp;

01:57:13.538 --> 01:57:18.440
Chris Beresford-Hill: So the reason I went&nbsp;
by the way too, was everyone, the pandemic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:18.440 --> 01:57:24.120
really, really was challenging. And I used to&nbsp;
like Balthazar, but I didn't love Balthazar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:24.120 --> 01:57:30.800
And when New York reopened, a lot of restaurants&nbsp;
popped out their al fresco, their outdoor cafes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:30.800 --> 01:57:34.680
and things like that. And I would always walk&nbsp;
by Balthazar and it was boarded up, boarded up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:34.680 --> 01:57:41.680
boarded up, and the owner, Keith, waited until you&nbsp;
could do full occupancy indoors before opening it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:41.680 --> 01:57:46.240
And when he opened it, he did a post. And I didn't&nbsp;
realize how much I missed it, but his post said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:46.240 --> 01:57:52.720
"Balthazar is open and it will never close&nbsp;
again." And coming out of that pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:52.720 --> 01:57:56.360
when everyone's like, "Everything's going to be&nbsp;
different," it'll never — just that statement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:56.360 --> 01:58:01.040
talk about communication, that kind of a subject&nbsp;
line on an email. I'm like, "I will go there every&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:01.040 --> 01:58:04.960
week then. As long as you're open, I'll go."
Tim Ferriss: This is going to sound like such&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:04.960 --> 01:58:11.640
a newbie question, but I'm curious, do you in&nbsp;
any way keep track of the ads or the messaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:11.640 --> 01:58:20.418
or the copy that snag you? Right? Because that's&nbsp;
a hell of a line. It's a good line. It worked.&nbsp;

01:58:20.418 --> 01:58:23.320
Chris Beresford-Hill: It absolutely worked.
Tim Ferriss: Do you have any, and these could&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:23.320 --> 01:58:28.440
be ideas that you come up with too, but do&nbsp;
you have any way of capturing ideas, whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:28.440 --> 01:58:34.178
they're yours or things you come across in the&nbsp;
wild where you're like, "Oh, wow, that's good."&nbsp;

01:58:34.178 --> 01:58:38.480
Chris Beresford-Hill: No, I'm an idiot. And I&nbsp;
forget. No, and I, probably 10 times a week I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:38.480 --> 01:58:42.960
see things where I'm like, "That's good." And then&nbsp;
I remember one of them, and if I was a smart man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:42.960 --> 01:58:46.400
I would have a notebook with me at all&nbsp;
times, or I would put it in my stickies&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:46.400 --> 01:58:50.080
and after this conversation, I'm going&nbsp;
to collect them because now I'm conscious&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:50.080 --> 01:58:54.160
because I see things all the time, and then&nbsp;
they come up at random times, but I can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:54.160 --> 01:58:59.520
call on them. So I would like to collect better.
Tim Ferriss: What I used to do, because I suppose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:59.520 --> 01:59:05.520
to invoke Glengarry Glen Ross, "You must get them&nbsp;
to sign on the line that is dotted." You can ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:05.520 --> 01:59:09.600
people if they're going to buy something,&nbsp;
but people are nice a lot of the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:09.600 --> 01:59:15.200
too nice. And they will tell you what they think&nbsp;
you want to hear. Getting someone to actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:15.200 --> 01:59:20.400
buy something is a different level of commitment.&nbsp;
It's a different type of signal. So when I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:20.400 --> 01:59:25.160
find myself compelled to buy something, especially&nbsp;
when I didn't have very much money and I was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:25.160 --> 01:59:30.440
getting started, cutting my teeth, trying to build&nbsp;
this direct response business in sports nutrition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:30.440 --> 01:59:38.425
if I bought something or if I got close to&nbsp;
buying it, I would save the ad or I would —&nbsp;

01:59:38.425 --> 01:59:39.680
Chris Beresford-Hill: Amazing.
Tim Ferriss: — I would take a video of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:39.680 --> 01:59:46.258
the infomercial or the commercial or I would take&nbsp;
notes on what it was that I thought got me to buy.&nbsp;

01:59:46.258 --> 01:59:47.120
Chris Beresford-Hill: What closed you.
Tim Ferriss: Right?&nbsp;

01:59:47.120 --> 01:59:49.480
Chris Beresford-Hill: That's really —
Tim Ferriss: Was it, "But wait, there's more;&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:49.480 --> 01:59:55.760
there's an installment?" Was it the timing, right?&nbsp;
Do I think it was just pure fatigue on my part&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:55.760 --> 02:00:01.200
where my defenses were low at 3:00 a.m. because&nbsp;
I'd been watching Animal Planet, and God knows&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:01.200 --> 02:00:09.000
some Ginsu knife thing came up and I would try to&nbsp;
figure out, although this was a secondary thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:09.000 --> 02:00:15.560
what had moved me to buy, especially as someone&nbsp;
who is not stingy but had a really tight budget&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:15.560 --> 02:00:19.160
for most things. And I no longer do that, but —
Chris Beresford-Hill: What did you learn?&nbsp;

02:00:19.160 --> 02:00:27.040
Tim Ferriss: I learned a lot about copy. At&nbsp;
the time, I was reading a lot of magazines&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:27.040 --> 02:00:36.840
and also newsletters and mostly text, because&nbsp;
text was easier to freeze and study. Just like&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:36.840 --> 02:00:41.480
for yourself, journaling is thoughts trapped&nbsp;
on paper. If you're trying to figure something&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:41.480 --> 02:00:45.520
out by sitting there and staring off into&nbsp;
space and thinking, sometimes it works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:45.520 --> 02:00:50.000
often it doesn't because it's too ephemeral.
These bits and pieces, the floats and jets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:50.000 --> 02:00:54.840
It's just like being thrown out of the boat in&nbsp;
the Grand Canyon and getting kind of tossed. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:54.840 --> 02:01:04.040
don't really — it's hard to orient to up when&nbsp;
you're in that type of chaotic mental state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:04.040 --> 02:01:07.600
even if it's just too much going&nbsp;
on, if you have a high-energy state.&nbsp;

02:01:07.600 --> 02:01:16.680
But for me, journaling for introspection, but&nbsp;
for external studying, it's not that text was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:16.680 --> 02:01:24.680
the best medium; it was just the easiest for&nbsp;
me to study as a fly locked in the amber.&nbsp;

02:01:24.680 --> 02:01:30.400
So what I learned was what kind of&nbsp;
headlines got me to look at anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:30.400 --> 02:01:37.080
right? So if I'm reading a magazine and especially&nbsp;
— I would read magazines I wasn't even interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:37.080 --> 02:01:39.720
in just to see what got my attention.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Interesting.&nbsp;

02:01:39.720 --> 02:01:43.800
Tim Ferriss: Not to really beat the drum on&nbsp;
Glengarry Glen Ross, which is actually a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:43.800 --> 02:01:50.600
depressing movie on a lot of levels when you get&nbsp;
to the bottom of it, but the "Attention, do I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:50.600 --> 02:01:57.040
your attention? Interested, are you interested?"
The AIDA: the first thing is attention. So I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:57.040 --> 02:02:03.000
pick up, say, a fashion magazine. I'm like, "All&nbsp;
right, this is going to be half ads," and I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:03.000 --> 02:02:08.000
just riff through. And I would see what would get&nbsp;
me to stop and I'd be like, "Okay, why did I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:08.000 --> 02:02:13.480
stop?" And I would tear it out of the magazine. It&nbsp;
might even be at a dentist's office or something.&nbsp;

02:02:13.480 --> 02:02:17.720
If I had downtime, I would just try to see&nbsp;
what would grab my attention and that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:17.720 --> 02:02:23.080
step one. So you learn a lot of things. I mean,&nbsp;
I don't want to go into a full monologue here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:23.080 --> 02:02:25.480
but I actually haven't talked about&nbsp;
this publicly, I don't think —&nbsp;

02:02:25.480 --> 02:02:30.960
First thing you realize is how important&nbsp;
art direction is: composition. Do you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:30.960 --> 02:02:36.680
a dominant element, or is everything the same?&nbsp;
If everything's the same, your eye isn't going&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:36.680 --> 02:02:40.720
to necessarily be drawn to any one component&nbsp;
and you're going to skip over it, generally.&nbsp;

02:02:40.720 --> 02:02:47.880
There are some weird exceptions where&nbsp;
the jumble is the branding. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:47.880 --> 02:02:54.120
I have used unscented baby soap from Dr. Bronner's&nbsp;
forever because it's just the least offensive to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:54.120 --> 02:03:00.960
my system and I really like it. Their branding,&nbsp;
if you look at the bottle, it's like size-two font&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:00.960 --> 02:03:03.240
with a million words covering the label.
Chris Beresford-Hill: I think I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:03.240 --> 02:03:07.760
that. Very super old-timey? Is that the —
Tim Ferriss: There may be some old-timey aspect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:07.760 --> 02:03:15.080
but it's a very small font. It's like&nbsp;
Unabomber, ransom-letter-y stuff. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:15.080 --> 02:03:20.800
you actually dig into it, it's not very easy&nbsp;
to follow all of it, but it's full of text.&nbsp;

02:03:20.800 --> 02:03:25.160
But it's like a horseshoe or it's — the&nbsp;
horseshoe is not the best way to explain it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:25.160 --> 02:03:32.680
but it's like you get to such a point of clutter&nbsp;
that it becomes a clear signal again, almost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:32.680 --> 02:03:37.320
as like a gestalt form of branding. So there are&nbsp;
some exceptions to that, but you realize, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:37.320 --> 02:03:41.218
okay." If you look at the Volkswagen ads,&nbsp;
if you look at some of the old classics.&nbsp;

02:03:41.218 --> 02:03:42.949
Chris Beresford-Hill: Very reductive.
Tim Ferriss: Reductive.&nbsp;

02:03:42.949 --> 02:03:44.160
Chris Beresford-Hill: Very —
Tim Ferriss: You're like, "Okay." Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:44.160 --> 02:03:47.200
I'm not going to get the ratio totally right,&nbsp;
but it's like, all right, the top two-thirds&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:47.200 --> 02:03:53.400
or three-quarters of this ad is a bold visual,&nbsp;
often black and white. There's one tagline that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:53.400 --> 02:03:59.640
like three to seven words, and then you've got&nbsp;
some copy at the bottom and a clip-out coupon or&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:59.640 --> 02:04:04.440
something for a lot of these direct-response ads.
So I studied the visual element because I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:04.440 --> 02:04:09.280
actually buying, and in fact, at the time, I was&nbsp;
designing a lot of my own print ads. So I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:09.280 --> 02:04:15.480
going into Photoshop and Illustrator and doing a&nbsp;
lot of the art. I was doing everything myself. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:15.480 --> 02:04:16.400
couldn't afford to hire anybody.
Chris Beresford-Hill: You&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:16.400 --> 02:04:24.160
were an advertising creative before I was.
Tim Ferriss: I learned a lot about clarity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:24.160 --> 02:04:29.480
also converting speeds and features into benefits.
Chris Beresford-Hill: But you said that the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:29.480 --> 02:04:34.520
things, and I mean these are the power of&nbsp;
art direction. To pull you into a world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:34.520 --> 02:04:40.160
to show you something that's interesting or&nbsp;
attractive is incredible. And it still feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:40.160 --> 02:04:44.560
like art. People, great art directors do not&nbsp;
do it scientifically. They have principles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:44.560 --> 02:04:49.680
but they're expressing and exploring.
And then the other thing is with also&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:49.680 --> 02:04:54.160
the magical set of words that can sell you.&nbsp;
Someone I used to work with, one of my mentors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:54.160 --> 02:04:59.360
Rob Schwartz, who used to be the chief&nbsp;
creative officer at TBWA in Los Angeles&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:59.360 --> 02:05:05.040
back in the day. He has a saying that I repeat&nbsp;
to everyone, which is: "Clients buy words."&nbsp;

02:05:05.720 --> 02:05:10.720
We can come to clients and assure them that&nbsp;
we can deliver across every social channel and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:10.720 --> 02:05:15.000
every asset and do all the strategy research,&nbsp;
but ultimately we're going to give them a set&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:15.000 --> 02:05:20.440
of words that tells them who they are, who they&nbsp;
can be, and who they are to their consumer. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:21.120 --> 02:05:26.280
those magical words are kind of everything. And&nbsp;
no matter what changes in the industry, clients&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:26.280 --> 02:05:31.520
buy words and consumers like me buy words too.
Tim Ferriss: Totally. And I feel like even if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:31.520 --> 02:05:38.280
aren't planning on playing offense in the sense&nbsp;
that you're not a writer, you're not crafting ads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:38.280 --> 02:05:45.400
I feel like everyone, certainly everyone who's&nbsp;
listening to this should make a deliberate&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:45.400 --> 02:05:52.680
study of words for defensive purposes.
Because if you are on social media or&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:52.680 --> 02:05:56.800
in any media, and of course, you are&nbsp;
exposed to advertising all the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:56.800 --> 02:06:02.320
you're exposed to communications all the&nbsp;
time, you can be manipulated very easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:02.320 --> 02:06:07.920
if you're not aware of how your mind is being&nbsp;
controlled or — it is being controlled. Informed.&nbsp;

02:06:08.480 --> 02:06:12.880
If I say, and this is cliche, but "Don't think of&nbsp;
a pink elephant, pink elephant, pink elephant."&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:14.560 --> 02:06:21.080
Your mind is very reactive in a lot of respects.&nbsp;
So I feel like the study of words is the study&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:21.080 --> 02:06:27.520
of thoughts and the malleability of thoughts,&nbsp;
which then leads to the malleability of behavior.&nbsp;

02:06:27.520 --> 02:06:32.960
I mean, if you really want to see how crazy&nbsp;
that can get, you can watch these Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:32.960 --> 02:06:40.480
specials like The Push from this illusionist&nbsp;
who's also an amazing graphic artist, or I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:40.480 --> 02:06:48.560
should say visual artist, painter, Derren Brown.
And these are long features that are mostly about&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:48.560 --> 02:06:54.440
social engineering, but just what you can get&nbsp;
someone to do if you profile them properly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:54.440 --> 02:06:59.360
have a delay, hire confederates. And&nbsp;
in this particular case, the push is:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:59.360 --> 02:07:05.400
can you get someone off the street and get them to&nbsp;
the point where they push someone off a building,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:05.400 --> 02:07:14.080
they commit murder just by scripting everything&nbsp;
out from start to finish? It is horrifying. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:14.080 --> 02:07:18.560
have very mixed feelings about it from an&nbsp;
ethical perspective. Nobody gets hurt. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:18.560 --> 02:07:24.360
has many more. Miracle, I think, is&nbsp;
another one, which is very well done.&nbsp;

02:07:24.360 --> 02:07:31.200
The point of all this is part of my fascination&nbsp;
with the Whipple book — part of my fascination&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:31.200 --> 02:07:36.840
with ads is for playing offense. So&nbsp;
building a business and so on. A lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:36.840 --> 02:07:44.240
it is just wanting to understand how humans work.
And the practitioners are always going to be ahead&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:44.240 --> 02:07:49.920
of the scientists and the theoreticians. That's&nbsp;
true in exercise where it's like the coaches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:49.920 --> 02:07:52.640
they're going to get a lot wrong. They're&nbsp;
going to get a lot right, and they're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:52.640 --> 02:07:58.680
to be able to see the state of the art before&nbsp;
the academics ever get their hands on it.&nbsp;

02:07:58.680 --> 02:08:02.800
So if you want to understand how humans work, one&nbsp;
approach, and these are not mutually exclusive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:02.800 --> 02:08:08.200
one approach is to study psychology and&nbsp;
so on. Another is to just look how people&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:08.200 --> 02:08:11.547
are affected and persuaded.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah.&nbsp;

02:08:11.547 --> 02:08:15.720
Tim Ferriss: So there are many books that I've&nbsp;
read, like — and I'm blanking on his first name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:15.720 --> 02:08:23.120
but Blunt is the last name, Words That Work, who&nbsp;
is, I believe, a Republican political strategist.&nbsp;

02:08:23.120 --> 02:08:26.880
And if I'm not mistaken, I read this a long time&nbsp;
ago. It was recommended to me by a very famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:26.880 --> 02:08:34.360
entrepreneur, and neither of us would say we have&nbsp;
much in common politically with this guy at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:34.360 --> 02:08:40.360
But he came up with as, one example, the death tax&nbsp;
instead of inheritance tax, like the death tax.&nbsp;

02:08:40.360 --> 02:08:44.960
Chris Beresford-Hill: Unappealing.
Tim Ferriss: And just these, a handful of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:44.960 --> 02:08:54.320
rebrands that became almost impossible to sort of&nbsp;
overturn in the zeitgeist. You should study that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:54.320 --> 02:08:57.320
right? There's a gold mine there&nbsp;
if you study that kind of thing.&nbsp;

02:08:57.320 --> 02:09:05.040
So you asked what I'd learned. I mean, I continue&nbsp;
to learn, I continue to study these things. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:05.040 --> 02:09:12.400
maybe I'm a weirdo, but it's so fundamental to&nbsp;
the existence — the day-to-day, week-to-week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:12.400 --> 02:09:16.520
month-to-month of existence of anyone listening&nbsp;
to this. And by the way, you're going to have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:16.520 --> 02:09:19.480
get a lot better at defense because —
Chris Beresford-Hill: Sure, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:19.480 --> 02:09:22.280
everything's going to get sharper.
Tim Ferriss: Everything — about to get so&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:22.280 --> 02:09:26.760
much overwhelming, so much more overwhelming, so&nbsp;
much more personalized. The number of pitches that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:26.760 --> 02:09:32.200
I get and the number of pitches my friends get now&nbsp;
just in the last few months where intro paragraphs&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:32.200 --> 02:09:36.280
are clearly crafted using AI, where it's —
Chris Beresford-Hill: It's going to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:36.280 --> 02:09:40.760
away my email writing gift. I better —
Tim Ferriss: I think it'll take a while.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:40.760 --> 02:09:45.160
It'll take a while because you can still pretty&nbsp;
easily discern, but you're going to have to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:45.160 --> 02:09:48.320
better at defense if you want to preserve&nbsp;
your sanity and your ability to function.&nbsp;

02:09:49.480 --> 02:09:56.360
So let me ask a question that has been in the&nbsp;
hopper for a little while. It's been gestating&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:56.360 --> 02:10:01.320
and it comes back to blue-sky thinking and&nbsp;
creative brainstorming and then constraints.&nbsp;

02:10:01.320 --> 02:10:06.640
Because listening to some of your stories and I'm&nbsp;
like, "Man, it'd be great to sit down for my own&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:06.640 --> 02:10:10.640
business and be like, 'You know what? I want to&nbsp;
get Bill Murray doing this in an ad. I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:10.640 --> 02:10:14.800
have him in the cockpit of a space shuttle.'"
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yes. I already&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:14.800 --> 02:10:19.400
want to watch this ad. I like it.
Tim Ferriss: And then there are certain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:19.400 --> 02:10:25.640
eventually, real-world considerations like&nbsp;
budget, like legal. And I'm wondering if&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:25.640 --> 02:10:31.560
there are examples of overriding these constraints&nbsp;
where it's worked out, or at what point you kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:31.560 --> 02:10:37.140
of bring things back to Earth, right? Because&nbsp;
if you do it too early, it would seem like —&nbsp;

02:10:37.140 --> 02:10:38.058
Chris Beresford-Hill: It stifles it.
Tim Ferriss: — it was it. Exactly.&nbsp;

02:10:38.058 --> 02:10:41.960
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah. There's no hard&nbsp;
and fast rules. I think there's — the nice&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:41.960 --> 02:10:46.360
thing about working at big agencies with big&nbsp;
brands is — my experience has always been:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:46.360 --> 02:10:49.960
if the idea is good enough, there's always&nbsp;
more money. I think I can say that without&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:49.960 --> 02:10:55.720
getting in trouble, but it's true.
And then I think creative people — we&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:55.720 --> 02:11:00.880
need the constraints to push off against —&nbsp;
to have ideas that have tension and that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:00.880 --> 02:11:05.080
going to be effective. So I think we try to&nbsp;
know as much about that going in as possible.&nbsp;

02:11:05.080 --> 02:11:09.640
And sometimes you'll have a $10&nbsp;
million idea for a $1 million budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:09.640 --> 02:11:15.080
and it's a tragedy because it would be amazing&nbsp;
and they would probably get great ROI on it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:15.080 --> 02:11:19.360
it might make economic sense, but I think —
Sorry, I'm quoting everyone and their mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:19.360 --> 02:11:25.240
but another one of my mentors, Rob Reilly, who's&nbsp;
the chief creative officer of the WPP Holding&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:25.240 --> 02:11:31.620
Company, always says, "No client ever fired&nbsp;
you for bringing extra ideas." And so I think —&nbsp;

02:11:31.620 --> 02:11:32.800
Tim Ferriss: That's a great line.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:32.800 --> 02:11:39.120
best things we've ever gotten away with have&nbsp;
been a lucky strike extra or saying, "Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:39.640 --> 02:11:43.800
here's the thing you asked for, but by the way, I&nbsp;
know this would involve getting a few celebrities&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:43.800 --> 02:11:48.800
or opening the coffers, but what about this?"
And I think once you've taken the defenses&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:48.800 --> 02:11:54.320
down by showing what's within possibility, you buy&nbsp;
yourself good will and permission to bring a crazy&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:54.320 --> 02:11:58.440
idea, and you're going to be sharing that crazy&nbsp;
idea with someone who's not nervous about what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:58.440 --> 02:12:03.080
to come; they're going to enjoy it. And so by&nbsp;
and large, you can get there with that. But yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:03.080 --> 02:12:07.947
I think you've got to cover your bases first&nbsp;
or else, yeah, they'll be pissed at you.&nbsp;

02:12:07.947 --> 02:12:10.947
Tim Ferriss: TBS' Funniest Ad of the Year.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yep.&nbsp;

02:12:10.947 --> 02:12:13.938
Tim Ferriss: Could you give us&nbsp;
some context on this prompt?&nbsp;

02:12:13.938 --> 02:12:17.280
Chris Beresford-Hill: This is — it's like&nbsp;
you're in my head, Tim, because you've just&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:17.280 --> 02:12:21.800
connected it and maybe you already know where&nbsp;
it's going, but my creative partner when I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:21.800 --> 02:12:27.280
at BBDO, there was a guy named Dan Lucey. And he&nbsp;
and I, he's my best friend. And for eight years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:27.280 --> 02:12:32.040
I spent more time with him than I spent with my&nbsp;
wife sharing an office and finishing each other's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:32.040 --> 02:12:37.680
sentences and fighting all the time too.
And we had a new client in Foot Locker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:37.680 --> 02:12:44.960
and their aspiration was to not be this kind of&nbsp;
a transactional house of brands, but for holding&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:44.960 --> 02:12:47.360
the Foot Locker bag to mean something.
And so we knew there was big ambition&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:47.360 --> 02:12:50.240
there that we had to — it wasn't just about the&nbsp;
shoes that were in stock; it was about trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:50.240 --> 02:12:54.640
make the brand cultural. So we worked together&nbsp;
on Foot Locker for about five or six years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:54.640 --> 02:12:59.240
and we created an ongoing campaign where we&nbsp;
got celebrities and athletes to make fun of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:59.240 --> 02:13:04.200
themselves and kind of play it close to the bone.
So part of this is we had a great client, a guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:04.200 --> 02:13:08.720
named Jed Berger, who — he didn't come up from&nbsp;
traditional marketing. He'd come from Modell's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:08.720 --> 02:13:11.098
which is an East Coast sports store.
Tim Ferriss: I know Modell's.&nbsp;

02:13:11.098 --> 02:13:12.220
Chris Beresford-Hill: You know&nbsp;
Modell's. We all know Modell's.&nbsp;

02:13:12.220 --> 02:13:13.600
Tim Ferriss: I grew up on Long Island.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah. But he also founded&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:13.600 --> 02:13:19.040
a basketball magazine called Dime Magazine. And&nbsp;
he had other interests besides just marketing. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:19.040 --> 02:13:24.720
he kind of came at things from a cultural place.
So sometimes we would show him ideas that got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:24.720 --> 02:13:29.720
the bone of sports issues or athlete issues,&nbsp;
and he always championed it. So this was our&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:29.720 --> 02:13:36.440
first kind of big campaign, and we overswung&nbsp;
the fences like crazy. There was maybe money&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:36.440 --> 02:13:43.640
for a cameo in an ad. And instead, we came up with&nbsp;
this cockamamie idea that for this one week, Foot&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:43.640 --> 02:13:49.240
Locker was putting out the very best shoes. They&nbsp;
were just going to stack it one day after another.&nbsp;

02:13:49.240 --> 02:13:52.920
And the client had called it the Week of&nbsp;
Greatness. And so we had written an ad that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:52.920 --> 02:13:57.480
said basically what's happening at Foot Locker, it&nbsp;
almost feels like with all these shoes coming out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:57.480 --> 02:14:03.080
it feels like all is right in the world. And then&nbsp;
we leave that scene and we go and we see a bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:03.080 --> 02:14:10.560
of wrongs be righted and impossibilities happen.
And so we wrote the script and it had Mike Tyson&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:10.560 --> 02:14:15.000
answering the door, and Evander Holyfield&nbsp;
is there with a box, and Mike Tyson returns&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:15.000 --> 02:14:20.560
the piece of the ear that he bit off Evander.
So already we're showing that to the rest of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:20.560 --> 02:14:24.760
team at BBDO. And they're like, "That's never&nbsp;
going to happen." And then we're like, "Don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:24.760 --> 02:14:28.894
worry, it keeps going." Dennis Rodman, this is —
Tim Ferriss: But wait, there's more.&nbsp;

02:14:28.894 --> 02:14:33.360
Chris Beresford-Hill: — 2012 kind of jokes. Dennis&nbsp;
Rodman, what did we all want to see? He was at an&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:33.360 --> 02:14:37.480
airline counter and he buys a one-way ticket to&nbsp;
North Korea. They're like, "That's never going&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:37.480 --> 02:14:41.720
to happen." And they're like, "But wait, there's&nbsp;
more." Because Brett Favre would refuse to retire.&nbsp;

02:14:41.720 --> 02:14:47.040
So Brett Favre is going to make a joke about&nbsp;
retiring. He's at a diner and he's got a piece&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:47.040 --> 02:14:52.160
of pie, and he hands it back to the lady&nbsp;
half-eaten and says, "It's time to stop."&nbsp;

02:14:52.160 --> 02:14:54.920
So one or the other, everyone said that,&nbsp;
"You can't bring this to them because this&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:54.920 --> 02:14:57.240
is going to cost a bajillion dollars.&nbsp;
They're never going to do it. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:57.240 --> 02:15:01.200
not — they asked us for something simple, and&nbsp;
you've given something incredibly complicated."&nbsp;

02:15:01.200 --> 02:15:09.080
And this is, I would say, kind of halfway through&nbsp;
my journey. And there's probably a period usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:09.080 --> 02:15:13.360
around the halfway point where you're maybe&nbsp;
a little more arrogant than you are later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:13.360 --> 02:15:17.920
in the beginning. And so Dan and I&nbsp;
just went, "Nah, we're going to do it."&nbsp;

02:15:17.920 --> 02:15:22.980
And so we got in such a blow-up fight&nbsp;
with our account lead that he said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:22.980 --> 02:15:25.080
"Well, you know what? You're going to tank&nbsp;
this whole thing and I'm not going to go to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:25.080 --> 02:15:29.800
the meeting." And so we went, "Sounds good."
So we went down to the meeting by ourselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:29.800 --> 02:15:35.520
without our business partner who forsook us&nbsp;
as a matter of pride and principle, and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:35.520 --> 02:15:39.480
presented it to this guy Jed, and he&nbsp;
went, "I fucking love it. Let's do it."&nbsp;

02:15:39.480 --> 02:15:45.560
So you've got to take your chances. There's&nbsp;
no shame in it. And we would've — and he knew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:45.560 --> 02:15:49.640
and Jed knew we would've had the back. We can&nbsp;
write him a cheap ad, but if we have a great one…&nbsp;

02:15:49.640 --> 02:15:55.760
And so he went to bat for… and he went and&nbsp;
he negotiated, our client negotiated with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:55.760 --> 02:15:59.880
all those athletes and people. And a little&nbsp;
side story, which for any sports nerds will&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:59.880 --> 02:16:04.680
be really fun is we filmed the Brett&nbsp;
Favre segment in Hattiesburg, Virginia.&nbsp;

02:16:04.680 --> 02:16:09.200
And so Dan went down there with the camera crew to&nbsp;
pick that up. He gave us half an hour because he&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:09.200 --> 02:16:15.600
was a high school football coach in his first&nbsp;
season, retired. And he famously had hung on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:15.600 --> 02:16:21.480
to things in Green Bay, and as the story goes,&nbsp;
prevented a young Aaron Rodgers from ascending and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:21.480 --> 02:16:25.440
taking the starting quarterback spot year after&nbsp;
year. And so there was tension there. And that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:25.440 --> 02:16:31.520
was famously, or in the press, a bad relationship.
And Dan calls me from set and he says, "I was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:31.520 --> 02:16:37.720
standing next to Brett Favre in between shots, and&nbsp;
I saw him open his fantasy football on his phone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:37.720 --> 02:16:44.480
and Aaron Rodgers is his starting quarterback."
And I was like, "God, that is such fun intel to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:44.479 --> 02:16:48.040
get from the front line." And I swear we were&nbsp;
like, "Should we call Bleacher Report and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:48.040 --> 02:16:52.947
tell them this because it's so…" People,&nbsp;
sports fans would've loved to know that.&nbsp;

02:16:52.947 --> 02:16:56.480
Tim Ferriss: I really have two questions.&nbsp;
There are 50 more. I'm just going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:56.479 --> 02:16:59.759
give some teasers that we won't get to.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yes. Why that? Why?&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:59.760 --> 02:17:02.787
What if that's something that someone really&nbsp;
wants to know about and then we didn't do it?&nbsp;

02:17:02.787 --> 02:17:04.560
Tim Ferriss: Well then maybe we do a round two.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:04.560 --> 02:17:07.640
fair enough, fair enough.
Tim Ferriss: So the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:07.640 --> 02:17:10.547
Kanye tried to get you fired.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yeah.&nbsp;

02:17:10.547 --> 02:17:12.081
Tim Ferriss: Okay. We're not going to do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:12.080 --> 02:17:18.000
although I really do at some point want to talk&nbsp;
about that because just the limited context&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:18.000 --> 02:17:22.600
that I have makes it seem really hilarious.
Chris Beresford-Hill: It was pretty good. Okay.&nbsp;

02:17:22.600 --> 02:17:27.360
Tim Ferriss: Okay. Actually, I'll let&nbsp;
you choose. This is going to be not&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:27.359 --> 02:17:29.987
dealer's choice. This will be your choice.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Okay. Gambler's choice.&nbsp;

02:17:29.987 --> 02:17:32.160
Tim Ferriss: So that's — gambler's&nbsp;
choice. This is one option.&nbsp;

02:17:32.160 --> 02:17:34.319
Chris Beresford-Hill: Okay.
Tim Ferriss: Another option is:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:34.319 --> 02:17:41.719
you on the cusp of taking your name off of the&nbsp;
Guinness wheelchair basketball ad and what that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:41.720 --> 02:17:45.960
was all about and just how that unfolded.
Chris Beresford-Hill: The Kanye is easy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:45.960 --> 02:17:50.920
but we'll save that for another time. The&nbsp;
wheelchair basketball one, because there was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:50.920 --> 02:17:55.399
good lesson in it, which is — so Dan, the partner&nbsp;
I mentioned, and another guy named Tom Kramer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:55.399 --> 02:18:00.817
I pitched that ad. And again —
Tim Ferriss: What is the ad?&nbsp;

02:18:00.818 --> 02:18:07.000
Chris Beresford-Hill: So in the ad, a group of&nbsp;
— it's almost like you start in a Nike ad or&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:07.000 --> 02:18:11.319
in a scene from the movie MegaBall$, which is a&nbsp;
movie that features wheelchair basketball. You're&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:11.319 --> 02:18:15.799
just in a really intense game, a wheelchair&nbsp;
basketball, and you get seduced into it. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:15.800 --> 02:18:20.160
follow the team down one end of the court to the&nbsp;
other, and you think it's all about performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:20.160 --> 02:18:26.120
or drive or what these guys are doing.
And as the game resolves, the surprise is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:26.120 --> 02:18:32.439
that nine out of the 10 guys get up out of their&nbsp;
chairs to leave the gymnasium, and then one friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:32.439 --> 02:18:38.040
is still in the chair. And that you understand&nbsp;
that this group of friends all participated&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:38.040 --> 02:18:42.846
in wheelchair basketball so that they could&nbsp;
play with their friend who's in a wheelchair.&nbsp;

02:18:42.846 --> 02:18:44.960
And the way we got to that — so the line&nbsp;
we had was, "Made of More," for Guinness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:44.960 --> 02:18:49.560
And it's simple. It's one of those great&nbsp;
lines where the beer itself is complicated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:49.560 --> 02:18:55.000
but also it's maybe a drink that maybe&nbsp;
someone who wants to feel like they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:55.000 --> 02:18:59.880
of more substance or that there's something&nbsp;
more substantial in that moment would choose.&nbsp;

02:18:59.880 --> 02:19:06.680
So the brief we gave ourselves was: what's a&nbsp;
story you would tell that you would wish you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:06.680 --> 02:19:10.640
could do that for a friend? Or if your friends&nbsp;
did that for you, that would mean everything?&nbsp;

02:19:10.640 --> 02:19:15.000
So it was just kind of writing that way. So it was&nbsp;
very simple, and that was what was pitched. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:15.000 --> 02:19:21.240
that's probably what anyone who saw the ad saw.&nbsp;
But what happened was, our experience making it&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:21.240 --> 02:19:27.439
was not great. We were pretty hard-headed at that.
So it was right around the Foot Locker time where&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:27.439 --> 02:19:34.519
we were maybe a little more rambunctious and&nbsp;
a little fighty. And we had a client that also&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:34.520 --> 02:19:42.041
was a little rambunctious and fighty. And I think&nbsp;
early on, for whatever reason, I think there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:42.040 --> 02:19:46.760
a sense of distrust that wasn't solved when we&nbsp;
were going to produce the ad. And for whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:46.760 --> 02:19:51.680
reason, we didn't quell it, we fought it.
So we brought forward the director we wanted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:51.680 --> 02:19:55.400
and the client didn't like something about&nbsp;
the director's treatment. The director will&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:55.399 --> 02:20:00.839
write a synopsis of how they planned to tell the&nbsp;
story. And instead of saying, "Okay, we hear you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:00.840 --> 02:20:05.280
let's go back and rewrite it," we tried to push&nbsp;
back. We tried to win the fight. And as I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:05.280 --> 02:20:10.399
I mentioned from my Adidas experience, you can't&nbsp;
really win these fights. You've got to play it&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:10.399 --> 02:20:15.160
collaboratively and forward. But we ended up —
Basically, every single thing was a fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:15.160 --> 02:20:21.519
and ultimately we lost every fight. And it got&nbsp;
to the point where the director was challenged&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:21.520 --> 02:20:27.121
and the casting was challenged. And the wardrobe&nbsp;
of one of the actors that wasn't even the lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:27.120 --> 02:20:31.559
actor was, "Why would you choose green shoes?"&nbsp;
And we like green shoes, but it didn't matter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:31.560 --> 02:20:35.760
but we lost. And then they weren't green shoes.
And there was a red stripe in the gym and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:35.760 --> 02:20:37.960
"Why is there a red stripe? That's&nbsp;
Budweiser's color." And we're like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:37.960 --> 02:20:41.640
"It doesn't matter." And then instead of just&nbsp;
saying, "We'll change it," we'll paint it out.&nbsp;

02:20:41.640 --> 02:20:45.040
So we fought everything. We lost everything,&nbsp;
and we just felt like such losers. By the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:45.040 --> 02:20:48.680
time we were done, the music wasn't&nbsp;
the track we liked, the set of words&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:48.680 --> 02:20:52.080
had to be rewritten because it wasn't right.
And we were so down and we were so miserable&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:52.080 --> 02:20:57.280
and sad that when it got released, we both said,&nbsp;
or the three of us said, "Listen, we're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:57.280 --> 02:21:04.920
take our names off it because this stank.&nbsp;
It's garbage," because maybe of our egos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:04.920 --> 02:21:08.120
the 30 battles we lost along the&nbsp;
way, that must've meant it was crap.&nbsp;

02:21:10.680 --> 02:21:13.360
Maybe it was subconscious, but we never made&nbsp;
that phone call, even though we intended to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:13.359 --> 02:21:17.479
I think we got busy, so we didn't ask for our&nbsp;
names to be taken off of it, but we intended&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:17.479 --> 02:21:23.759
to. And then it aired with no fanfare on a playoff&nbsp;
baseball game in October that I wasn't watching.&nbsp;

02:21:23.760 --> 02:21:32.040
The ad was not on YouTube. Someone videoed&nbsp;
it on their phone, maybe on their DVR,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:32.040 --> 02:21:35.080
uploaded it to YouTube, and by the morning it had&nbsp;
over a million views. But I don't even know how&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:35.080 --> 02:21:38.840
anyone found it. I don't even know how you could&nbsp;
search it, but it just kind of went through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:38.840 --> 02:21:42.480
roof because it touched the thing that we&nbsp;
wanted to touch where everyone felt, "God,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:42.479 --> 02:21:46.599
I would do that for someone. I pray I have&nbsp;
people around me that would do that for me."&nbsp;

02:21:46.600 --> 02:21:50.140
But we missed the whole point because we were so&nbsp;
caught up in the 30 things that we lost, we missed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:50.140 --> 02:21:54.360
— the story was still intact and it was still&nbsp;
good, but we were obsessed about our own personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:54.359 --> 02:21:59.080
experience on it that made us lose faith in it.
And so it's just that constant reminder to step&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:59.080 --> 02:22:02.680
back, ask yourself if you're feeling a&nbsp;
certain way — does it really matter? Is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:02.680 --> 02:22:07.080
really changing things or are you just caught&nbsp;
in the weeds? So that was a big lesson for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:07.080 --> 02:22:15.200
to get outside of my own attitude about stuff.
Tim Ferriss: Were you able to change your behavior&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:15.200 --> 02:22:18.267
in future projects because of that?
Chris Beresford-Hill: I think so.&nbsp;

02:22:18.267 --> 02:22:18.800
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Chris Beresford-Hill: I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:18.800 --> 02:22:23.440
think so. I think it was a clear lesson&nbsp;
that a lot of things we thought matter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:23.439 --> 02:22:30.346
don't matter. So maybe we need to be a little more&nbsp;
amenable to feedback and suggestion. You know?&nbsp;

02:22:30.346 --> 02:22:32.240
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, totally.
Chris Beresford-Hill: But it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:32.240 --> 02:22:38.200
good. There was a work lesson and a life lesson.
Tim Ferriss: So lessons, not that you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:38.200 --> 02:22:43.399
impart a lesson, but the billboard question.&nbsp;
So if you could put a message, a quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:43.399 --> 02:22:48.719
an image, anything on a billboard, non-commercial,&nbsp;
metaphorically speaking, just to get something in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:48.720 --> 02:22:54.080
front of a lot of people, what would you put?
Chris Beresford-Hill: First thing is I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:54.080 --> 02:22:57.519
request the billboard location would be at&nbsp;
my house because I want to see it every day&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:57.520 --> 02:23:01.520
because I'm — whatever matters to me is probably&nbsp;
the thing that I need to be reminded of more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:01.520 --> 02:23:07.760
I need to broadcast. But I would just say,&nbsp;
in the spirit of creativity and living an&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:07.760 --> 02:23:10.578
interesting life, "See what's possible."
Tim Ferriss: "See what's possible."&nbsp;

02:23:10.578 --> 02:23:14.000
Chris Beresford-Hill: "See what's possible."
Tim Ferriss: What would that mean to you if&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:14.000 --> 02:23:17.120
it were in front of your house?&nbsp;
How would that be helpful to you?&nbsp;

02:23:17.120 --> 02:23:22.000
Chris Beresford-Hill: It would remind me&nbsp;
every day that I'm not living a routine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:22.000 --> 02:23:29.160
but I'm here to see what I can do and what&nbsp;
people around me can do. And how can we — I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:29.160 --> 02:23:33.519
don't like the word maximize because that sounds&nbsp;
maybe a little functional, but how can you fully&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:33.520 --> 02:23:39.480
express in life by taking chances, by believing&nbsp;
in yourself, by trying new things? And it can mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:39.479 --> 02:23:44.359
anything to anyone, but it's just that reminder&nbsp;
of, "I'm not here to do what the plan is today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:44.359 --> 02:23:49.157
I'm here to see what's possible."
Tim Ferriss: I love it. Chris —&nbsp;

02:23:49.157 --> 02:23:49.960
Chris Beresford-Hill: Tim.
Tim Ferriss: — we are winding to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:49.960 --> 02:23:54.760
close. Is there anything else that you would&nbsp;
like to — well, first of all, any websites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:54.760 --> 02:23:57.880
places to point people to that you'd like&nbsp;
to mention? And then is there anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:57.880 --> 02:24:02.800
you'd like to say before we come to a close?
Chris Beresford-Hill: Well, first of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:02.800 --> 02:24:09.279
thank you. The best place to find me is LinkedIn,&nbsp;
because I tend to post a lot about creativity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:09.279 --> 02:24:16.040
work and work that we are doing and work that I&nbsp;
find inspiring. So it's a good place to engage.&nbsp;

02:24:16.040 --> 02:24:22.920
And I hope anyone out there — we were talking&nbsp;
before we started that when I was 21, a career&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:22.920 --> 02:24:28.240
in advertising was one of the few options for&nbsp;
a creative person. Today, you can self-publish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:28.240 --> 02:24:33.160
you can become an influencer, you can write&nbsp;
your own movie, you can publish your own novel.&nbsp;

02:24:33.160 --> 02:24:40.599
It's not as attractive, but I hope we continue&nbsp;
to attract the best talent. And I hope people&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:40.600 --> 02:24:45.640
do pursue it because it's a really fun job. You&nbsp;
learn about all kinds of different businesses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:45.640 --> 02:24:49.840
They tell you all their secrets about why things&nbsp;
aren't working, how good their product really is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:49.840 --> 02:24:54.680
and you get to come up with ideas to solve it.
And if you hit it, you get to put them out&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:54.680 --> 02:24:59.800
into the world and get your ideas in front of,&nbsp;
hopefully, millions of people. And I think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:59.800 --> 02:25:05.400
probably a career that doesn't seem as illustrious&nbsp;
as it used to be, but my hope is that we continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:05.399 --> 02:25:10.120
to bring great people in because that's the&nbsp;
only way it's going to be vibrant and exciting.&nbsp;

02:25:10.120 --> 02:25:13.960
And by the way, otherwise, you're just going to&nbsp;
have horrible ads on TV or whatever screen we look&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:13.960 --> 02:25:17.840
at. So it's kind of like, "Let's get good people&nbsp;
in this industry so that we don't have to look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:17.840 --> 02:25:20.120
horrible marketing in the future."
Tim Ferriss: And you also get&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:20.120 --> 02:25:21.279
to collaborate, right?
Chris Beresford-Hill: Yes.&nbsp;

02:25:21.279 --> 02:25:25.679
Tim Ferriss: It's a big one. I mean, a lot of&nbsp;
people out there I think have lost sight of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:25.680 --> 02:25:31.600
the possibility of collaborating in these types of&nbsp;
environments and these types of companies because&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:31.600 --> 02:25:37.080
the shiny object for a lot of young people&nbsp;
is running your own ship as an influencer.&nbsp;

02:25:37.080 --> 02:25:43.960
But there are a lot of trade-offs, and one of them&nbsp;
that I know from having spoken to a lot of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:43.960 --> 02:25:50.891
who are creators independently is you can end up&nbsp;
being or feeling very isolated and very lonely.&nbsp;

02:25:50.891 --> 02:25:53.680
Chris Beresford-Hill: That's right.
Tim Ferriss: And one of my top priorities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:53.680 --> 02:25:56.760
in fact, I just finished an&nbsp;
offsite recently for my whole team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:56.760 --> 02:26:03.800
was fast creative collaborations with&nbsp;
world-class people. Because you can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:03.800 --> 02:26:07.400
a lot as a solo operator, even with&nbsp;
a team, but if you're in isolation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:07.399 --> 02:26:13.160
you're not going to be broadening, at least I&nbsp;
don't think, for me personally, your horizons.&nbsp;

02:26:13.160 --> 02:26:19.200
You're not going to be bending and testing your&nbsp;
perception of the world in a way that you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:19.200 --> 02:26:27.559
in a collaborative environment. So we will link&nbsp;
to your LinkedIn in the show notes for everyone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:27.560 --> 02:26:30.640
and we will link to everything we've&nbsp;
discussed in the show notes everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:30.640 --> 02:26:39.100
at tim.blog/podcast. That'll be easy to find.
And until next time, please&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:39.100 --> 02:26:51.255
be a little bit kinder than is necessary,&nbsp;
not just to others, but to yourself. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:51.255 --> 02:26:55.488
until next time, thanks for tuning in.
Chris Beresford-Hill: Awesome. Thanks.

